  ('28015f6662d8cc0750a6646e99a53dd9e3bc929f0cf1ed0ba105b835c33ed3be', '起きて、朝ごはんを食べて、学校に行きます.', '[{"t":"起","r":"お"},{"t":"きて"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝","r":"あさ"},{"t":"ごはんを"},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べて"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"きます"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e5d5fced891f530fef117767de838c44cd8f371c4d5b0e7ae6c3fa747b542046', 'The dictionary form (辞書形) is the verb as you would find it in a dictionary: 食べる, 書く, する. It always ends in a う-row syllable.

It is not only a citation form. It is a real, usable tense (the plain non-past), meaning both "eat" and "will eat" depending on context. 明日行く is a perfectly complete sentence meaning "I''ll go tomorrow"; it is simply casual where 行きます is polite.

Every drill in this app hands you the dictionary form and asks for something else, because that is the direction the language works in: 書く is the root, 書きます and 書いて and 書いた are all derived from it.', '[{"t":"The dictionary form ("},{"t":"辞書","r":"じしょ"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":") is the verb as you would find it in a dictionary: "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べる"},{"t":", "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"く"},{"t":", "},{"t":"する"},{"t":". It always ends in a "},{"t":"う"},{"t":"-row syllable.\n\nIt is not only a citation form. It is a real, usable tense (the plain non-past), meaning both \"eat\" and \"will eat\" depending on context. "},{"t":"明日","r":"あした"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is a perfectly complete sentence meaning \"I''ll go tomorrow\"; it is simply casual where "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"きます"},{"t":" is polite.\n\nEvery drill in this app hands you the dictionary form and asks for something else, because that is the direction the language works in: "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is the root, "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"きます"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"いて"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":" are all derived from it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('fac387f658ed8b5354261624c46a23a97a8aebe89f132c12d7af081a649c611f', 'Japanese has no infinitive. English "to eat" and "eat" are separate ideas; 食べる is both, plus "will eat". Which one it means is decided by the sentence around it, never by the verb itself.', '[{"t":"Japanese has no infinitive. English \"to eat\" and \"eat\" are separate ideas; "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べる"},{"t":" is both, plus \"will eat\". Which one it means is decided by the sentence around it, never by the verb itself."}]'::jsonb),
  ('da1617739ecda71a6728a5c775f64b183d1a05be589476984d295a2a97c1f00c', '日本に行ったことがあります "I have been to Japan".', '[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"ったことがあります"},{"t":" \"I have been to Japan\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0589e6a0777c43b50d69ff715d2fcbb4dcccf87f42b37268022370750f2dc261', 'Experience, not past tense. 日本に行きました says you went; 行ったことがある says it is among the things you have done. Not usable for something that happened once, recently.', '[{"t":"Experience, not past tense. "},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"きました"},{"t":" says you went; "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"ったことがある"},{"t":" says it is among the things you have done. Not usable for something that happened once, recently."}]'::jsonb),
