  ('3f88f153ffbbcdb97602ac1f9555cc84922a2e44c37eafba41784f33913c0b6d', '筈 is the notch at the end of an arrow, cut to fit the bowstring.', '[{"t":"筈"},{"t":" is the notch at the end of an arrow, cut to fit the bowstring."}]'::jsonb),
  ('aa5b2e9ff18be174bb90e5689ecb428c6e7f4b89e0cb96e8b2f53d3ab8ee5f2b', 'A 筈 is made to fit. Nock the arrow and it seats, because that is what it was shaped for.

So はず is not a guess. It is the expectation you have when the parts were made to go together: 彼は来るはずだ says the arrangement was made, not that you feel he probably will. That is the whole difference from かもしれない and でしょう, and why はずがない is a flat contradiction rather than a doubt.', '[{"t":"A "},{"t":"筈"},{"t":" is made to fit. Nock the arrow and it seats, because that is what it was shaped for.\n\nSo "},{"t":"はず"},{"t":" is not a guess. It is the expectation you have when the parts were made to go together: "},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来","r":"く"},{"t":"るはずだ"},{"t":" says the arrangement was made, not that you feel he probably will. That is the whole difference from "},{"t":"かもしれない"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"でしょう"},{"t":", and why "},{"t":"はずがない"},{"t":" is a flat contradiction rather than a doubt."}]'::jsonb),
  ('35c636459b2b29627fb8f0e0cb3942e8542c6a6b5ff41e36ffe69908a2eaa430', '易い and 難い are ordinary adjectives meaning easy and difficult.', '[{"t":"易","r":"やさ"},{"t":"い"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"難","r":"にく"},{"t":"い"},{"t":" are ordinary adjectives meaning easy and difficult."}]'::jsonb),
  ('62bf657eae8dc26acb763c39df889e294d651082241f961689ab71dd114eca08', 'Both stand alone in older and formal Japanese, and both keep their own inflection in the compound (読みやすかった, 読みにくくない), because an adjective is what they still are.

難い is worth following further. It survives in 有り難い, "difficult to exist", said of something so rare it is precious; and that word, worn down, is ありがとう. The word for thank you is an adjective meaning hard to come by.', '[{"t":"Both stand alone in older and formal Japanese, and both keep their own inflection in the compound ("},{"t":"読","r":"よ"},{"t":"みやすかった"},{"t":", "},{"t":"読","r":"よ"},{"t":"みにくくない"},{"t":"), because an adjective is what they still are.\n\n"},{"t":"難","r":"にく"},{"t":"い"},{"t":" is worth following further. It survives in "},{"t":"有","r":"あ"},{"t":"り"},{"t":"難","r":"にく"},{"t":"い"},{"t":", \"difficult to exist\", said of something so rare it is precious; and that word, worn down, is "},{"t":"ありがとう"},{"t":". The word for thank you is an adjective meaning hard to come by."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a946e37aaeb00ce2f522ba181b0a9901cda7619ee0c1db3a850be0912459b0f8', 'Classical Japanese had a 終止形 to end a sentence and a 連体形 to stand before a noun, and for most verbs they differed. They collapsed together, and the surviving shape is the one dictionaries list.

Japanese never needed a relative pronoun: even before the merger the 連体形 modified nouns directly. What the merger did was hand both jobs to one shape, so 食べる now ends a sentence and also sits straight in front of a noun: 食べる人.', '[{"t":"Classical Japanese had a "},{"t":"終","r":"お"},{"t":"止","r":"と"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" to end a sentence and a "},{"t":"連","r":"つ"},{"t":"体","r":"たい"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" to stand before a noun, and for most verbs they differed. They collapsed together, and the surviving shape is the one dictionaries list.\n\nJapanese never needed a relative pronoun: even before the merger the "},{"t":"連","r":"つ"},{"t":"体","r":"たい"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" modified nouns directly. What the merger did was hand both jobs to one shape, so "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べる"},{"t":" now ends a sentence and also sits straight in front of a noun: "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べる"},{"t":"人","r":"と"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('20029599f241621bb6867ad3901be7e74796d8df0156fa52f4fbd1a1b03702bd', 'Classical Japanese had なる as an attributive copula (に plus あり), and 静かなる夜 was "a night that is quiet". な is what is left of it.

Everything odd about the class follows. They take だ and でした because nouns do; they take な before a noun because that is the copula''s modifying form; and 静かの has to be 静かな for the same reason. The class is noun-like and leans on the copula for everything, which is why it patterns with nouns nearly everywhere, and yet takes な where a true noun takes の.', '[{"t":"Classical Japanese had "},{"t":"なる"},{"t":" as an attributive copula ("},{"t":"に"},{"t":" plus "},{"t":"あり"},{"t":"), and "},{"t":"静","r":"しず"},{"t":"かなる"},{"t":"夜","r":"よる"},{"t":" was \"a night that is quiet\". "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" is what is left of it.\n\nEverything odd about the class follows. They take "},{"t":"だ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"でした"},{"t":" because nouns do; they take "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" before a noun because that is the copula''s modifying form; and "},{"t":"静","r":"しず"},{"t":"かの"},{"t":" has to be "},{"t":"静","r":"しず"},{"t":"かな"},{"t":" for the same reason. The class is noun-like and leans on the copula for everything, which is why it patterns with nouns nearly everywhere, and yet takes "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" where a true noun takes "},{"t":"の"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('cd3ebdf39fa19444ec481fc410997720b2cbac3eb240e87909e0e7a684fe4e57', 'Classical Japanese had 係り結び: certain particles mid-sentence forced a particular ending on the final verb, and こそ demanded the 已然形. The agreement is gone; the particle stayed.

