  ('9ea69035f7b80a18f0026dee1760139b9647fef9cd03dfa57aaa9ea3edd357a7', '関する is a Sino-Japanese verb, "to relate to".', '[{"t":"関","r":"かん"},{"t":"する"},{"t":" is a Sino-Japanese verb, \"to relate to\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4959efdcb81fddd1deb5f44b5f447e50c719299937b4c57c0b829b869d5c5982', 'It enters through kanbun, and に関して is the written register''s について, the difference being only that 関する is Sino-Japanese and 付く is native, so the first belongs to reports and the second to speech.

The 関 also used to write native かかわる is spelling, not kinship: かかわる is its own word, written 係わる and 拘わる just as readily. And neither carries the settled dispute of をめぐって.', '[{"t":"It enters through kanbun, and "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"関","r":"かか"},{"t":"して"},{"t":" is the written register''s "},{"t":"について"},{"t":", the difference being only that "},{"t":"関","r":"かん"},{"t":"する"},{"t":" is Sino-Japanese and "},{"t":"付","r":"づ"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is native, so the first belongs to reports and the second to speech.\n\nThe "},{"t":"関","r":"かか"},{"t":" also used to write native "},{"t":"かかわる"},{"t":" is spelling, not kinship: "},{"t":"かかわる"},{"t":" is its own word, written "},{"t":"係","r":"がかり"},{"t":"わる"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"拘わる"},{"t":" just as readily. And neither carries the settled dispute of "},{"t":"をめぐって"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d7b8c9267c328db246ff40ee01eed09d092c81443dcd6ef26c99108cb4943c71', 'It inflects like an adjective: ごとく connects onward, ごとき stands before a noun, ごとし ends a sentence. 光陰矢のごとし, time flies like an arrow, is the phrase everybody knows.

Modern ようだ replaced it entirely, which is why ごとき now sounds either literary or contemptuous. 私ごとき is self-deprecation precisely because reaching for the archaic form makes a show of the comparison.', '[{"t":"It inflects like an adjective: "},{"t":"ごとく"},{"t":" connects onward, "},{"t":"ごとき"},{"t":" stands before a noun, "},{"t":"ごとし"},{"t":" ends a sentence. "},{"t":"光","r":"ひかり"},{"t":"陰","r":"いん"},{"t":"矢","r":"や"},{"t":"のごとし"},{"t":", time flies like an arrow, is the phrase everybody knows.\n\nModern "},{"t":"ようだ"},{"t":" replaced it entirely, which is why "},{"t":"ごとき"},{"t":" now sounds either literary or contemptuous. "},{"t":"私","r":"わちき"},{"t":"ごとき"},{"t":" is self-deprecation precisely because reaching for the archaic form makes a show of the comparison."}]'::jsonb),
  ('1e50f2ddc44c8a7f9dd430942cf74dcea9c01ccb2caa3086ad6e6aa35e105edd', 'It is the opposite arrow from 上げる, and it is why くれる cannot be used for a gift between two other people that has nothing to do with you: the verb encodes arrival at your side. The spelling 呉れる is ateji: 呉 writes the sound and is not where the verb comes from, which is obscure.

Its honorific is 下さる, literally "to give downward", which is the 下さい you have been saying since the first lesson. 買ってくれた and 買ってくださった are one verb at two heights.', '[{"t":"It is the opposite arrow from "},{"t":"上","r":"あ"},{"t":"げる"},{"t":", and it is why "},{"t":"くれる"},{"t":" cannot be used for a gift between two other people that has nothing to do with you: the verb encodes arrival at your side. The spelling "},{"t":"呉れる"},{"t":" is ateji: "},{"t":"呉"},{"t":" writes the sound and is not where the verb comes from, which is obscure.\n\nIts honorific is "},{"t":"下","r":"くだ"},{"t":"さる"},{"t":", literally \"to give downward\", which is the "},{"t":"下","r":"くだ"},{"t":"さい"},{"t":" you have been saying since the first lesson. "},{"t":"買","r":"か"},{"t":"ってくれた"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"買","r":"か"},{"t":"ってくださった"},{"t":" are one verb at two heights."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b9b9515e894927e01b7125167b52dfd75c472fa31486374f8d80f9ed99f1b470', 'ください is the imperative of 下さる, an honorific verb meaning "to give downward".', '[{"t":"ください"},{"t":" is the imperative of "},{"t":"下","r":"くだ"},{"t":"さる"},{"t":", an honorific verb meaning \"to give downward\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b93ad1f0a7ffb2f388a16b2a660c88b241269949d3445e9836c2b6a0cc2197f9', 'It is written 下さい: the kanji 下 "below" is doing real work. 下さる is the honorific of くれる ("to give to me"), and it pictures the giving as coming DOWN from someone above you to you below.

So てください literally asks someone to bestow the action downward on you. That is why it stays polite but still directive: you are describing a gift from a superior, not making a tentative request.', '[{"t":"It is written "},{"t":"下","r":"くだ"},{"t":"さい"},{"t":": the kanji "},{"t":"下","r":"した"},{"t":" \"below\" is doing real work. "},{"t":"下","r":"くだ"},{"t":"さる"},{"t":" is the honorific of "},{"t":"くれる"},{"t":" (\"to give to me\"), and it pictures the giving as coming DOWN from someone above you to you below.\n\nSo "},{"t":"てください"},{"t":" literally asks someone to bestow the action downward on you. That is why it stays polite but still directive: you are describing a gift from a superior, not making a tentative request."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4871f1121d7b18ce0e3a72cfe57226648b13ba0a946ea6259537cc7e7633ace9', 'It is written 間, the same character read かん in 時間 and 人間, but that is a Sino-Japanese morpheme and あいだ is the older native word underneath the shared spelling. 間に applies it to time: the stretch between one point and another.

The に is what makes it a window rather than a duration. 寝ている間 is the whole time; 寝ている間に is some moment inside it, which is why the second takes a one-off event and the first takes something lasting.', '[{"t":"It is written "},{"t":"間","r":"かん"},{"t":", the same character read "},{"t":"かん"},{"t":" in "},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"人間","r":"にんげん"},{"t":", but that is a Sino-Japanese morpheme and "},{"t":"あいだ"},{"t":" is the older native word underneath the shared spelling. "},{"t":"間","r":"ま"},{"t":"に"},{"t":" applies it to time: the stretch between one point and another.\n\nThe "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" is what makes it a window rather than a duration. "},{"t":"寝","r":"ね"},{"t":"ている"},{"t":"間","r":"かん"},{"t":" is the whole time; "},{"t":"寝","r":"ね"},{"t":"ている"},{"t":"間","r":"ま"},{"t":"に"},{"t":" is some moment inside it, which is why the second takes a one-off event and the first takes something lasting."}]'::jsonb),
  ('fdb8a610c05c071f5dab0de0c6b827eb53fe5a2c67ab1b1a27a38fec303c5f7e', 'It looks like する and is not. す／さす attached to the 未然形 and meant to make or let happen; it survives as modern せる／させる.

Make and let are one form because the auxiliary never separated them. With an intransitive verb the particle can tilt it (子供を行かせる leans toward compelling, 子供に行かせる toward permitting), but with a transitive verb the causee takes に either way (子供にご飯を食べさせる), and context decides.', '[{"t":"It looks like "},{"t":"する"},{"t":" and is not. "},{"t":"す"},{"t":"／"},{"t":"さす"},{"t":" attached to the "},{"t":"未","r":"ま"},{"t":"然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" and meant to make or let happen; it survives as modern "},{"t":"せる"},{"t":"／"},{"t":"させる"},{"t":".\n\nMake and let are one form because the auxiliary never separated them. With an intransitive verb the particle can tilt it ("},{"t":"子供","r":"こども"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"かせる"},{"t":" leans toward compelling, "},{"t":"子供","r":"こども"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"かせる"},{"t":" toward permitting), but with a transitive verb the causee takes "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" either way ("},{"t":"子供","r":"こども"},{"t":"にご"},{"t":"飯","r":"はん"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べさせる"},{"t":"), and context decides."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b71d5f8796d1f77d61862e81291aa8514a7a3a84c34b2503e747d8e8e02fb063', 'It marks the sentence as new information being given, which is why it can sound helpful (落ちましたよ), and equally why it can sound like being told off.

The risk is telling somebody what they already know. 会議は三時ですよ to the person who scheduled it is grammatical and rude, because よ has claimed they needed telling.', '[{"t":"It marks the sentence as new information being given, which is why it can sound helpful ("},{"t":"落","r":"お"},{"t":"ちましたよ"},{"t":"), and equally why it can sound like being told off.\n\nThe risk is telling somebody what they already know. "},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"三","r":"さん"},{"t":"時","r":"とき"},{"t":"ですよ"},{"t":" to the person who scheduled it is grammatical and rude, because "},{"t":"よ"},{"t":" has claimed they needed telling."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f4e009d512eec3b396267a68b88d9d2f805dc3ddc33fe8d6c834a9224e6d9513', '同じ behaves like nothing else because it is a leftover from an older adjective class.', '[{"t":"同","r":"おな"},{"t":"じ"},{"t":" behaves like nothing else because it is a leftover from an older adjective class."}]'::jsonb),
  ('6935cc01cc18764c1c202bce2e80f12b39197c034df8096e5600860ee6e88dc9', 'It modifies a noun bare, 同じ本, where a な-adjective would need な and an i-adjective would end in い. Grammars class it as a 連体詞 for that reason.

But it takes だ and です like a な-adjective, and 同じで links like one. It is a word caught between two classes, and the irregularity is worth learning as a fact rather than a rule.', '[{"t":"It modifies a noun bare, "},{"t":"同","r":"おな"},{"t":"じ"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":", where a "},{"t":"な"},{"t":"-adjective would need "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" and an i-adjective would end in "},{"t":"い"},{"t":". Grammars class it as a "},{"t":"連","r":"つ"},{"t":"体","r":"たい"},{"t":"詞"},{"t":" for that reason.\n\nBut it takes "},{"t":"だ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"です"},{"t":" like a "},{"t":"な"},{"t":"-adjective, and "},{"t":"同","r":"おな"},{"t":"じで"},{"t":" links like one. It is a word caught between two classes, and the irregularity is worth learning as a fact rather than a rule."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f60848b443c376c7f60a63596e8ee61798e06bbcfcdf8115c4b91fc88f310d57', 'It pairs the words with the saying of them: 「はい」と言った. Nothing marks the quotation as speech; と simply joins the content to the verb.

That is why と works for thought as readily as speech: と思う, と考える. It is also why INDIRECT quotation drops to the plain form: when you report the content rather than the words, politeness belongs to the sentence around it. A direct quote keeps whatever was actually said, 「はい、そうです」と言った included.', '[{"t":"It pairs the words with the saying of them: 「"},{"t":"はい"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"った"},{"t":". Nothing marks the quotation as speech; "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" simply joins the content to the verb.\n\nThat is why "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" works for thought as readily as speech: "},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思","r":"おも"},{"t":"う"},{"t":", "},{"t":"と"},{"t":"考","r":"かんが"},{"t":"える"},{"t":". It is also why INDIRECT quotation drops to the plain form: when you report the content rather than the words, politeness belongs to the sentence around it. A direct quote keeps whatever was actually said, 「"},{"t":"はい"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうです"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"った"},{"t":" included."}]'::jsonb),
  ('481d5662b5cca1eda72bbdde52f3a365466e011568099e5c61cc5b48b9f4c700', 'Japanese splits existence in two: いる for what moves of itself, ある for what does not. 〜てある is built on the inanimate one, so what it asserts is that a thing exists in a done condition.

That explains the particle switch that makes the pattern look irregular. 窓を開けてある becomes 窓が開けてある because the sentence is no longer about anyone opening anything; it is about the window, and what is being said of it is that it stands opened. The person who did it has been left out on purpose, and ている differs exactly there: 開いている says the window is open, with nobody implied at all.', '[{"t":"Japanese splits existence in two: "},{"t":"いる"},{"t":" for what moves of itself, "},{"t":"ある"},{"t":" for what does not. 〜"},{"t":"てある"},{"t":" is built on the inanimate one, so what it asserts is that a thing exists in a done condition.\n\nThat explains the particle switch that makes the pattern look irregular. "},{"t":"窓","r":"まど"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開","r":"あ"},{"t":"けてある"},{"t":" becomes "},{"t":"窓","r":"まど"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開","r":"あ"},{"t":"けてある"},{"t":" because the sentence is no longer about anyone opening anything; it is about the window, and what is being said of it is that it stands opened. The person who did it has been left out on purpose, and "},{"t":"ている"},{"t":" differs exactly there: "},{"t":"開","r":"あ"},{"t":"いている"},{"t":" says the window is open, with nobody implied at all."}]'::jsonb),
  ('9998e370173af18bd9e0cf689b57e72b9a4389d2de71bb2ad98580ea93d49f6c', 'Old Japanese had both: ば on the 未然形 was hypothetical, ば on the 已然形 meant "when" or "because": something taken as actually so. The 未然形 construction died out, and the surviving 已然形 one took over the hypothetical meaning. Modern grammar renames that stem 仮定形, "hypothetical form", which quietly records the swap.

The old realis sense has not entirely gone. It is why ば sits so comfortably in general truths (春になれば暖かくなる), and why a command after an action-verb ば-clause sounds wrong where たら is fine. After a stative or adjectival condition it is unremarkable: 時間があれば来てください.', '[{"t":"Old Japanese had both: "},{"t":"ば"},{"t":" on the "},{"t":"未","r":"ま"},{"t":"然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" was hypothetical, "},{"t":"ば"},{"t":" on the "},{"t":"已然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" meant \"when\" or \"because\": something taken as actually so. The "},{"t":"未","r":"ま"},{"t":"然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" construction died out, and the surviving "},{"t":"已然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" one took over the hypothetical meaning. Modern grammar renames that stem "},{"t":"仮定","r":"かてい"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":", \"hypothetical form\", which quietly records the swap.\n\nThe old realis sense has not entirely gone. It is why "},{"t":"ば"},{"t":" sits so comfortably in general truths ("},{"t":"春","r":"はる"},{"t":"になれば"},{"t":"暖","r":"あたた"},{"t":"かくなる"},{"t":"), and why a command after an action-verb "},{"t":"ば"},{"t":"-clause sounds wrong where "},{"t":"たら"},{"t":" is fine. After a stative or adjectival condition it is unremarkable: "},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん"},{"t":"があれば"},{"t":"来","r":"らい"},{"t":"てください"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('facd178e4b76e14dcfd4c23ad24c7e3f8fd4830e8e559dc995b71d1f7625b2e2', 'The potential and the passive are the same auxiliary, which is why 食べられる is both.', '[{"t":"The potential and the passive are the same auxiliary, which is why "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べられる"},{"t":" is both."}]'::jsonb),
  ('848426e3c8a7ecbbf29fd328d2b295510dbf2012ccce2d1b156d4ac9e00e600e', 'Old Japanese had one auxiliary, る／らる, carrying four senses at once: passive, potential, spontaneous and honorific. Modern れる／られる inherited all four, undivided.

Godan verbs escaped. Somewhere around the Edo period they grew a dedicated potential of their own (書ける, 読める, the 可能動詞), and stopped using 書かれる to mean "can write". Ichidan verbs and 来る never did, which is exactly why 食べられる still has to carry both jobs and 書ける does not.', '[{"t":"Old Japanese had one auxiliary, "},{"t":"る"},{"t":"／"},{"t":"らる"},{"t":", carrying four senses at once: passive, potential, spontaneous and honorific. Modern "},{"t":"れる"},{"t":"／"},{"t":"られる"},{"t":" inherited all four, undivided.\n\nGodan verbs escaped. Somewhere around the Edo period they grew a dedicated potential of their own ("},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"ける"},{"t":", "},{"t":"読","r":"よ"},{"t":"める"},{"t":", the "},{"t":"可能","r":"かのう"},{"t":"動詞","r":"どうし"},{"t":"), and stopped using "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"かれる"},{"t":" to mean \"can write\". Ichidan verbs and "},{"t":"来","r":"く"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" never did, which is exactly why "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べられる"},{"t":" still has to carry both jobs and "},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"ける"},{"t":" does not."}]'::jsonb),
  ('141d45b079f687807ff2d28c6cefead2a7f99ffb2bb36bc6aeeafaa2c66ffcd7', 'Old Japanese verbs had a 命令形 among their six forms, and modern 行け and 食べろ come straight down from it. It is a conjugation, not something added to a verb.

There is nowhere in it to put politeness: that is what ください and なさい are for, and both are built from other verbs entirely. It is also why the imperative survives where bluntness is the point: signs, coaching, and 頑張れ.', '[{"t":"Old Japanese verbs had a "},{"t":"命令","r":"めいれい"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" among their six forms, and modern "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"け"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べろ"},{"t":" come straight down from it. It is a conjugation, not something added to a verb.\n\nThere is nowhere in it to put politeness: that is what "},{"t":"ください"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"なさい"},{"t":" are for, and both are built from other verbs entirely. It is also why the imperative survives where bluntness is the point: signs, coaching, and "},{"t":"頑"},{"t":"張","r":"は"},{"t":"れ"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('adbe1562d9c0c82c7ba9348ee6addf868b3166adbc73333f2738f502292e7069', 'Old Japanese was verb-final and so is the modern language; nothing in twelve hundred years moved it. What comes before the verb is comparatively free, because the particles say what each phrase is doing rather than the position.

That is the trade: English fixes the order and drops the case marking, Japanese fixes the verb and marks everything else. It is why 私はパンを食べます and パンを私は食べます are both grammatical. In writing the particles are what cannot be spared; speech drops は and を freely (これ食べる？), because the context is doing their work.', '[{"t":"Old Japanese was verb-final and so is the modern language; nothing in twelve hundred years moved it. What comes before the verb is comparatively free, because the particles say what each phrase is doing rather than the position.\n\nThat is the trade: English fixes the order and drops the case marking, Japanese fixes the verb and marks everything else. It is why "},{"t":"私","r":"わちき"},{"t":"はパンを"},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べます"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"パンを"},{"t":"私","r":"わちき"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べます"},{"t":" are both grammatical. In writing the particles are what cannot be spared; speech drops "},{"t":"は"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"を"},{"t":" freely ("},{"t":"これ"},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べる"},{"t":"？), because the context is doing their work."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a7df1a2204c8015fa374bf2c7d1f0570335f7b167af68dd3aa68c522bf611794', 'Old Japanese さへ added something on top of what was already there. The modern sense is that addition narrowed to its most surprising case: the one you would not have added.

The "if only" use came from elsewhere. In classical Japanese that job belonged to a different particle (だに, 命だに〜ば), and さえ took over だに''s territory in the medieval period, which is how one particle ended up carrying both "even" and "if only". Reading 来さえすればいい as "if only the coming is added" is a serviceable mnemonic but not the history.', '[{"t":"Old Japanese "},{"t":"さへ"},{"t":" added something on top of what was already there. The modern sense is that addition narrowed to its most surprising case: the one you would not have added.\n\nThe \"if only\" use came from elsewhere. In classical Japanese that job belonged to a different particle ("},{"t":"だに"},{"t":", "},{"t":"命","r":"いのち"},{"t":"だに"},{"t":"〜"},{"t":"ば"},{"t":"), and "},{"t":"さえ"},{"t":" took over "},{"t":"だに"},{"t":"''s territory in the medieval period, which is how one particle ended up carrying both \"even\" and \"if only\". Reading "},{"t":"来","r":"らい"},{"t":"さえすればいい"},{"t":" as \"if only the coming is added\" is a serviceable mnemonic but not the history."}]'::jsonb),
  ('7b04d0b5c7bd4a319d192c8a354a5c5ad00d8943015f06e2c8c1d2b54146ef0a', 'らしい grew out of the adjectival suffix of 男らしい, not directly from the Old Japanese auxiliary らし.', '[{"t":"らしい"},{"t":" grew out of the adjectival suffix of "},{"t":"男","r":"だん"},{"t":"らしい"},{"t":", not directly from the Old Japanese auxiliary "},{"t":"らし"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d8605139442daacffe93267cec5651ffba1ee0311d7b109ece7c6158d1cad485', 'Old Japanese らし did mark inference from evidence, but it fell out of the spoken language by mid-Heian and was gone by Kamakura. Modern conjectural らしい arose in the Edo period out of the Late Middle Japanese SUFFIX らしい, the one in 男らしい and 学生らしい, "as one would expect of", echoing the remembered classical auxiliary rather than continuing it.

The suffix is therefore the source and the conjecture the offshoot, which is the reverse of how the two are usually presented. Both are still one idea: judging something by the evidence of what it appears to be. That is the difference from ようだ, which rests on what the speaker makes of it.', '[{"t":"Old Japanese "},{"t":"らし"},{"t":" did mark inference from evidence, but it fell out of the spoken language by mid-Heian and was gone by Kamakura. Modern conjectural "},{"t":"らしい"},{"t":" arose in the Edo period out of the Late Middle Japanese SUFFIX "},{"t":"らしい"},{"t":", the one in "},{"t":"男","r":"だん"},{"t":"らしい"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい"},{"t":"らしい"},{"t":", \"as one would expect of\", echoing the remembered classical auxiliary rather than continuing it.\n\nThe suffix is therefore the source and the conjecture the offshoot, which is the reverse of how the two are usually presented. Both are still one idea: judging something by the evidence of what it appears to be. That is the difference from "},{"t":"ようだ"},{"t":", which rests on what the speaker makes of it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3811335d99227834a3f42a7a5539af10c360a77b02245299729df1d09041a041', 'One auxiliary, four senses, none of them lost. It is why 先生が来られる can mean the teacher was come to, can come, or, most likely, simply comes, spoken respectfully.

One influential account ties the suffering passive to the spontaneous sense rather than the passive one: 雨に降られた would then be the older idea of something happening of itself, to someone, which explains the grievance and the fact that an intransitive verb can be passivised at all. Others read it as a later stretch of the plain passive with an affected party added. The construction is certain; its route is not.', '[{"t":"One auxiliary, four senses, none of them lost. It is why "},{"t":"先生","r":"せんじょう"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来","r":"らい"},{"t":"られる"},{"t":" can mean the teacher was come to, can come, or, most likely, simply comes, spoken respectfully.\n\nOne influential account ties the suffering passive to the spontaneous sense rather than the passive one: "},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"降","r":"お"},{"t":"られた"},{"t":" would then be the older idea of something happening of itself, to someone, which explains the grievance and the fact that an intransitive verb can be passivised at all. Others read it as a later stretch of the plain passive with an affected party added. The construction is certain; its route is not."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f55c7130a3964b3ff98234c0491cfa205bf539d1c0cc48db1377f89b10902806', '中 read ちゅう is in the middle of something; read じゅう it is throughout.', '[{"t":"中","r":"なか"},{"t":" read "},{"t":"ちゅう"},{"t":" is in the middle of something; read "},{"t":"じゅう"},{"t":" it is throughout."}]'::jsonb),
  ('dc60f8232889bb16508589d902ef8f1a541da21f92fe78827e670a6acdfd1870', 'One character, two on-readings, and they do not mean the same thing. 授業中 is during the lesson; 一日中 is all day long.

The split is not predictable from the character, so the reading has to be learned with each word. Roughly, ちゅう takes an activity you are in the middle of and じゅう a span or space filled completely: 世界中, 一年中.', '[{"t":"One character, two on-readings, and they do not mean the same thing. "},{"t":"授業","r":"じゅぎょう"},{"t":"中","r":"なか"},{"t":" is during the lesson; "},{"t":"一日","r":"いちにち"},{"t":"中","r":"なか"},{"t":" is all day long.\n\nThe split is not predictable from the character, so the reading has to be learned with each word. Roughly, "},{"t":"ちゅう"},{"t":" takes an activity you are in the middle of and "},{"t":"じゅう"},{"t":" a span or space filled completely: "},{"t":"世界","r":"せかい"},{"t":"中","r":"なか"},{"t":", "},{"t":"一年","r":"いちねん"},{"t":"中","r":"なか"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('23f85cbb2e28107613d3782823fe768b7b24e279f3192ba7efbe3b3436dfd5b3', '上 as a suffix is the same 上 as 上で and 上に, read じょう.', '[{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":" as a suffix is the same "},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":" as "},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":"で"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":", read "},{"t":"じょう"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('144c0bd257758521798f804bf736b9cc9b8eec26232a33d7c7d5fe5e98991a13', 'One kanji across three patterns at this level. As a Sino-Japanese suffix it means "on the plane of": 法律上 is on the legal plane, 健康上 on the health one.

It favours compact, compound-ready nouns, overwhelmingly Sino-Japanese, though modern loans join freely enough, as セキュリティ上の理由 and ルール上 show.', '[{"t":"One kanji across three patterns at this level. As a Sino-Japanese suffix it means \"on the plane of\": "},{"t":"法律","r":"ほうりつ"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":" is on the legal plane, "},{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":" on the health one.\n\nIt favours compact, compound-ready nouns, overwhelmingly Sino-Japanese, though modern loans join freely enough, as "},{"t":"セキュリティ"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"理由","r":"りゆう"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"ルール"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":" show."}]'::jsonb),
  ('31fb7b502c6780dad86e3fc8fbb2c4eef35f3558d2d610fec95687d6a987f26b', 'The chain is て + あり → たり → た. It was originally a resultative ("having done X, and being in that state") rather than a past tense.

That is why た still leans perfect in places where English would not use a past: 分かった for "I understand (now)", or the surprised あった when you find something. The form is telling you a state has been reached, not merely that time has passed.', '[{"t":"The chain is "},{"t":"て"},{"t":" + "},{"t":"あり"},{"t":" → "},{"t":"たり"},{"t":" → "},{"t":"た"},{"t":". It was originally a resultative (\"having done X, and being in that state\") rather than a past tense.\n\nThat is why "},{"t":"た"},{"t":" still leans perfect in places where English would not use a past: "},{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"かった"},{"t":" for \"I understand (now)\", or the surprised "},{"t":"あった"},{"t":" when you find something. The form is telling you a state has been reached, not merely that time has passed."}]'::jsonb),
  ('249750f3d200d9f81e8b6a189e5d534ec31c7ec287d90753680a19c78ded8da9', 'The chain is です + う, spelled でせう before the kana reform. The plain だろう is the parallel formation on である (であらう), and the two are often run together, but they are built on different copulas.

The final う is the same volitional ending that gives 行こう, which is why でしょう guesses rather than states: the volitional was never about certainty. It marks the speaker projecting rather than reporting, covering both "probably" and the seeking-agreement 〜でしょう？ with no change of form.', '[{"t":"The chain is "},{"t":"です"},{"t":" + "},{"t":"う"},{"t":", spelled "},{"t":"でせう"},{"t":" before the kana reform. The plain "},{"t":"だろう"},{"t":" is the parallel formation on "},{"t":"である"},{"t":" ("},{"t":"であらう"},{"t":"), and the two are often run together, but they are built on different copulas.\n\nThe final "},{"t":"う"},{"t":" is the same volitional ending that gives "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"こう"},{"t":", which is why "},{"t":"でしょう"},{"t":" guesses rather than states: the volitional was never about certainty. It marks the speaker projecting rather than reporting, covering both \"probably\" and the seeking-agreement 〜"},{"t":"でしょう"},{"t":"？ with no change of form."}]'::jsonb),
  ('85b61c22be310f9be58725d7254352818f7c7db953645ec90ab1ed13750d7ea8', 'The dictionaries offer several accounts: an old noun of reason or interval, a formal noun, or the から of 人柄 and 家柄, a nature or stock. None has settled it.

What is not in doubt is that the two uses are one word. A journey from Tokyo and a decision from a reason are the same idea: the thing it started out of. That is why Japanese needs no separate word for "because" here, and why から sits after a noun or a whole clause without changing shape.', '[{"t":"The dictionaries offer several accounts: an old noun of reason or interval, a formal noun, or the "},{"t":"から"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"人柄","r":"ひとがら"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"家","r":"け"},{"t":"柄","r":"え"},{"t":", a nature or stock. None has settled it.\n\nWhat is not in doubt is that the two uses are one word. A journey from Tokyo and a decision from a reason are the same idea: the thing it started out of. That is why Japanese needs no separate word for \"because\" here, and why "},{"t":"から"},{"t":" sits after a noun or a whole clause without changing shape."}]'::jsonb),
  ('879e69209300ba84c5e6275d5464001f9219226be171234ae512e452a1b9d547', 'The full phrase means "whether thus or otherwise": in any case, no matter what. Worn down to とても it kept that shrug, and とても行けない, "there is no way I can go", is the original construction and still current.

The positive とても寒い spread in Taishō and Shōwa. Akutagawa called it a 新流行 and said it was not Tokyo language; Tsubouchi called it 訛語. They lost, but the negative use never went anywhere, which is why とても〜ない reads as idiomatic rather than as a contradiction.', '[{"t":"The full phrase means \"whether thus or otherwise\": in any case, no matter what. Worn down to "},{"t":"とても"},{"t":" it kept that shrug, and "},{"t":"とても"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"けない"},{"t":", \"there is no way I can go\", is the original construction and still current.\n\nThe positive "},{"t":"とても"},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"い"},{"t":" spread in Taishō and Shōwa. Akutagawa called it a "},{"t":"新","r":"あら"},{"t":"流行","r":"りゅうこう"},{"t":" and said it was not Tokyo language; Tsubouchi called it "},{"t":"訛"},{"t":"語","r":"かた"},{"t":". They lost, but the negative use never went anywhere, which is why "},{"t":"とても"},{"t":"〜"},{"t":"ない"},{"t":" reads as idiomatic rather than as a contradiction."}]'::jsonb),
  ('ee0bcd3dab521bb99444a127125bfa2cb59113021751564f86a042c1f5f51b2b', '兼ねる is "to hold two things at once"; strapped to a verb it became "cannot quite manage it".', '[{"t":"兼","r":"か"},{"t":"ねる"},{"t":" is \"to hold two things at once\"; strapped to a verb it became \"cannot quite manage it\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c5dce31168f93d13c6c1d2a542a4d59174d8d46315ead145430a86ed351bf360', 'The independent verb still means combining: 大臣を兼ねる. The auxiliary shifted from juggling two things to failing to manage one, and it is already in the Man''yōshū: 忘れかねつ.

かねない is that auxiliary negated (not-unable), hence "might well". That is the whole reason two forms one syllable apart mean opposite things, and why you only say a bad thing is not impossible when you are worried about it.', '[{"t":"The independent verb still means combining: "},{"t":"大臣","r":"だいじん"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"兼","r":"か"},{"t":"ねる"},{"t":". The auxiliary shifted from juggling two things to failing to manage one, and it is already in the Man''yōshū: "},{"t":"忘","r":"わす"},{"t":"れかねつ"},{"t":".\n\n"},{"t":"かねない"},{"t":" is that auxiliary negated (not-unable), hence \"might well\". That is the whole reason two forms one syllable apart mean opposite things, and why you only say a bad thing is not impossible when you are worried about it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('2e8f05b95370f0a0fe70484f549d208074e9044bff3ad66e84ceef58ad0ade81', 'The job has not changed in twelve hundred years: put の between two nouns and the first modifies the second. Old Japanese had other particles that did the same work (が among them, which is why 我が国 still reads as "my country"), and の is the one that stayed general.', '[{"t":"The job has not changed in twelve hundred years: put "},{"t":"の"},{"t":" between two nouns and the first modifies the second. Old Japanese had other particles that did the same work ("},{"t":"が"},{"t":" among them, which is why "},{"t":"我","r":"わ"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"国","r":"くに"},{"t":" still reads as \"my country\"), and "},{"t":"の"},{"t":" is the one that stayed general."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d9754ee0fa05c6f947b0855f3cca1e79755847b485d13e7cc4d95b177f8fb65b', 'The mirror of お〜になる. Your act stays an act (nothing is softened about the doing), and the humility comes from the お, which frames the action as offered toward the person it touches.

That direction is the rule the pattern lives by: it only works for something done to or for somebody else. お待ちします is waiting for you; there is no honest お〜する for eating your own lunch, because there is nobody for the act to be pointed at.', '[{"t":"The mirror of "},{"t":"お"},{"t":"〜"},{"t":"になる"},{"t":". Your act stays an act (nothing is softened about the doing), and the humility comes from the "},{"t":"お"},{"t":", which frames the action as offered toward the person it touches.\n\nThat direction is the rule the pattern lives by: it only works for something done to or for somebody else. "},{"t":"お"},{"t":"待","r":"ま"},{"t":"ちします"},{"t":" is waiting for you; there is no honest "},{"t":"お"},{"t":"〜"},{"t":"する"},{"t":" for eating your own lunch, because there is nobody for the act to be pointed at."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4399a277c2e9de0155d88c685e112c97a38570703e9c482ea02865ad79e9db42', 'まま is 儘, a noun meaning a state left exactly as it stood.', '[{"t":"まま"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"儘"},{"t":", a noun meaning a state left exactly as it stood."}]'::jsonb),
  ('848c3def8228ddb784820d212e5f9f830875fec5c2b64d67d81bb7d114c59cbd', 'The noun survives in わがまま, "one''s own way", and in 気の向くまま. It names a condition nobody has altered.

That is why the resultative use takes た: 開けたまま names a state produced by a finished act, where English says "with the window open". The noun itself takes other states just as happily: 知らないまま, and the 気の向くまま above.', '[{"t":"The noun survives in "},{"t":"わがまま"},{"t":", \"one''s own way\", and in "},{"t":"気","r":"き"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"向","r":"む"},{"t":"くまま"},{"t":". It names a condition nobody has altered.\n\nThat is why the resultative use takes "},{"t":"た"},{"t":": "},{"t":"開","r":"あ"},{"t":"けたまま"},{"t":" names a state produced by a finished act, where English says \"with the window open\". The noun itself takes other states just as happily: "},{"t":"知","r":"し"},{"t":"らないまま"},{"t":", and the "},{"t":"気","r":"き"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"向","r":"む"},{"t":"くまま"},{"t":" above."}]'::jsonb),
  ('9b9aaf980c6c4ccae1b53dace576dfde8e99e3980369511ebf1c227042b67b26', 'The particle attaches to a plain clause and adds it to a case being built. 安いし、近いし says two things in support of a conclusion the speaker may never reach out loud.

That is why a single し sounds unfinished (one reason offered as though there were others) and why し so often ends a sentence with the rest left hanging. Its origin is not settled, but its job in the modern language is.', '[{"t":"The particle attaches to a plain clause and adds it to a case being built. "},{"t":"安","r":"やす"},{"t":"いし"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"近","r":"ちか"},{"t":"いし"},{"t":" says two things in support of a conclusion the speaker may never reach out loud.\n\nThat is why a single "},{"t":"し"},{"t":" sounds unfinished (one reason offered as though there were others) and why "},{"t":"し"},{"t":" so often ends a sentence with the rest left hanging. Its origin is not settled, but its job in the modern language is."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c9f32ea89e2265dc8133fd9e6f917b36d41e40c98b26c752b387aa7187e82113', 'The particle is the same one in 私も and 一度も. It adds the sense of even, and nothing else has been added anywhere.

雨が降っても行く is therefore "even with it raining, I go"; the te-form supplies the linking, も supplies the concession. It also explains the question-word pattern 何を食べても, where も keeps its sweeping sense and takes the whole range at once.', '[{"t":"The particle is the same one in "},{"t":"私","r":"わちき"},{"t":"も"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど"},{"t":"も"},{"t":". It adds the sense of even, and nothing else has been added anywhere.\n\n"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降","r":"お"},{"t":"っても"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is therefore \"even with it raining, I go\"; the te-form supplies the linking, "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" supplies the concession. It also explains the question-word pattern "},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べても"},{"t":", where "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" keeps its sweeping sense and takes the whole range at once."}]'::jsonb),
  ('235fb4e1b851f876eaa6af29c53f81e60cb1badef969080d497b64110e4985d9', 'と思う is the quotative と with 思う, exactly as と言う is with 言う.', '[{"t":"と"},{"t":"思","r":"おも"},{"t":"う"},{"t":" is the quotative "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" with "},{"t":"思","r":"おも"},{"t":"う"},{"t":", exactly as "},{"t":"と"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"う"},{"t":" is with "},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"う"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c9408d1daad4b3a94aaf9da5940fedda43e53feaf44a554d09763aa287a3f90e', 'The particle pairs the thought with the thinking of it. Japanese does not distinguish saying from thinking at the level of grammar; both take と and both take the plain form in front.

That is why politeness belongs outside: 行くと思います, never 行きますと思います. What you are reporting is the thought itself, and the thought was not phrased politely to yourself.', '[{"t":"The particle pairs the thought with the thinking of it. Japanese does not distinguish saying from thinking at the level of grammar; both take "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" and both take the plain form in front.\n\nThat is why politeness belongs outside: "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"くと"},{"t":"思","r":"おも"},{"t":"います"},{"t":", never "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"きますと"},{"t":"思","r":"おも"},{"t":"います"},{"t":". What you are reporting is the thought itself, and the thought was not phrased politely to yourself."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c564209df10bed25c8c093913362906684369ae097945d6bec96b74c0fafbefe', 'The prohibitive な is Old Japanese and attaches to the plain present: 行くな, "do not go". The gentle な is a contraction of なさい and attaches to the ます stem: 行きな, "go on then".

So 食べるな and 食べな are opposites separated by one syllable, and the syllable is not the な; it is what the な is stuck to. Reading the stem tells you which one you are looking at, and nothing else will.', '[{"t":"The prohibitive "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" is Old Japanese and attaches to the plain present: "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"くな"},{"t":", \"do not go\". The gentle "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" is a contraction of "},{"t":"なさい"},{"t":" and attaches to the "},{"t":"ます"},{"t":" stem: "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"きな"},{"t":", \"go on then\".\n\nSo "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べるな"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べな"},{"t":" are opposites separated by one syllable, and the syllable is not the "},{"t":"な"},{"t":"; it is what the "},{"t":"な"},{"t":" is stuck to. Reading the stem tells you which one you are looking at, and nothing else will."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b89c0783f1736836bcf71364b916f219782d699eb52c359ffdeb4976c915c981', 'The same adjective behind ない, なしに and なしには. なくして is the adverbial なく with the classical connective して (the same して as in にして), since なく plus て would only give なくて.

努力なくして成功はない demands the negative after it for the same reason なしには does: the absence has been set up as the thing the sentence is about, and only a denial completes it.', '[{"t":"The same adjective behind "},{"t":"ない"},{"t":", "},{"t":"なしに"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"なしには"},{"t":". "},{"t":"なくして"},{"t":" is the adverbial "},{"t":"なく"},{"t":" with the classical connective "},{"t":"して"},{"t":" (the same "},{"t":"して"},{"t":" as in "},{"t":"にして"},{"t":"), since "},{"t":"なく"},{"t":" plus "},{"t":"て"},{"t":" would only give "},{"t":"なくて"},{"t":".\n\n"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく"},{"t":"なくして"},{"t":"成功","r":"せいこう"},{"t":"はない"},{"t":" demands the negative after it for the same reason "},{"t":"なしには"},{"t":" does: the absence has been set up as the thing the sentence is about, and only a denial completes it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('770d2cff8bfeb395a9fcc9045322a29def80953bb91fb31569866bcbfe880175', 'に限り draws the boundary that に限らず denies.', '[{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"り"},{"t":" draws the boundary that "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"らず"},{"t":" denies."}]'::jsonb),
  ('57706ae57b0ee5d22a73fd10b5aa3b1f7e133d956507b2418e2f045151bb59d2', 'The same 限る. 本日に限り says today is the edge and nothing outside it counts.

That is why it belongs to notices and offers (学生に限り半額), where the point is exactly where the line falls. 〜に限る, the other form, means something else entirely: 夏はビールに限る, "nothing beats", which is the limit read as the only thing worth having.', '[{"t":"The same "},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"る"},{"t":". "},{"t":"本日","r":"ほんじつ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"り"},{"t":" says today is the edge and nothing outside it counts.\n\nThat is why it belongs to notices and offers ("},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"り"},{"t":"半","r":"はん"},{"t":"額","r":"がく"},{"t":"), where the point is exactly where the line falls. 〜"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", the other form, means something else entirely: "},{"t":"夏","r":"か"},{"t":"はビールに"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", \"nothing beats\", which is the limit read as the only thing worth having."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0f1a65590b386533063428a1d1557f578efc4aa9ca3d96e43ffed981f95bf875', 'がたい is classical 難し; にくい is a different word entirely, from 憎い.', '[{"t":"がたい"},{"t":" is classical "},{"t":"難","r":"むずか"},{"t":"し"},{"t":"; "},{"t":"にくい"},{"t":" is a different word entirely, from "},{"t":"憎","r":"にく"},{"t":"い"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e472026122fb67dd9ecdeafe3ccfd2b26af51a4d0867a993858f861c855a45ba', 'The shared kanji 難 is spelling, not shared origin. がたし meant difficult; the suffix にくい comes from 憎い, "disagreeable", extended to "hard to"; dictionaries gloss it 《難い・悪い》 and trace it to 憎む.

The division of labour follows: にくい is physical awkwardness (読みにくい字), がたい is difficulty in bringing yourself to it (信じがたい, 忘れがたい), and がたい keeps to written and emotional contexts because 難し is the older, stiffer word. 難い in ありがたい (hard to exist, therefore precious) is where ありがとう comes from.', '[{"t":"The shared kanji "},{"t":"難","r":"なん"},{"t":" is spelling, not shared origin. "},{"t":"がたし"},{"t":" meant difficult; the suffix "},{"t":"にくい"},{"t":" comes from "},{"t":"憎","r":"にく"},{"t":"い"},{"t":", \"disagreeable\", extended to \"hard to\"; dictionaries gloss it 《"},{"t":"難","r":"にく"},{"t":"い・"},{"t":"悪","r":"わる"},{"t":"い"},{"t":"》 and trace it to "},{"t":"憎","r":"にく"},{"t":"む"},{"t":".\n\nThe division of labour follows: "},{"t":"にくい"},{"t":" is physical awkwardness ("},{"t":"読","r":"よ"},{"t":"みにくい"},{"t":"字","r":"あざな"},{"t":"), "},{"t":"がたい"},{"t":" is difficulty in bringing yourself to it ("},{"t":"信","r":"しん"},{"t":"じがたい"},{"t":", "},{"t":"忘","r":"わす"},{"t":"れがたい"},{"t":"), and "},{"t":"がたい"},{"t":" keeps to written and emotional contexts because "},{"t":"難","r":"むずか"},{"t":"し"},{"t":" is the older, stiffer word. "},{"t":"難","r":"にく"},{"t":"い"},{"t":" in "},{"t":"ありがたい"},{"t":" (hard to exist, therefore precious) is where "},{"t":"ありがとう"},{"t":" comes from."}]'::jsonb),
  ('158581d903efd3278a8441dc3c79023c6f788171c1358c420163d87a658cda0d', 'とはいえ is と with は and いえ, the classical imperative-shaped 已然形 of 言う.', '[{"t":"とはいえ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" with "},{"t":"は"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"いえ"},{"t":", the classical imperative-shaped "},{"t":"已然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"う"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0b24fc71748e58bbd3b8f9d3a8a26aaa3acafb1cfa69a0652adbd9ed4f4d37bf', 'いく and くる are 行く and 来る, and they point away from now and toward it.', '[{"t":"いく"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"くる"},{"t":" are "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"来","r":"く"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", and they point away from now and toward it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('33aae7cdfe86a36f66cdca153b62cfde36c224affe835c49f61e8975d6dba523', 'The two verbs of motion, doing in time what they do in space. 来る arrives where the speaker is; 行く departs from there.

Set the anchor at the present moment and the whole pattern falls out. 増えてきた is an increase that has travelled up to now, so it looks back; 増えていく carries on past now, so it looks forward. It is also why 〜てくる so often covers a beginning; the movement had to start somewhere behind you to have reached here.', '[{"t":"The two verbs of motion, doing in time what they do in space. "},{"t":"来","r":"く"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" arrives where the speaker is; "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" departs from there.\n\nSet the anchor at the present moment and the whole pattern falls out. "},{"t":"増","r":"ふ"},{"t":"えてきた"},{"t":" is an increase that has travelled up to now, so it looks back; "},{"t":"増","r":"ふ"},{"t":"えていく"},{"t":" carries on past now, so it looks forward. It is also why 〜"},{"t":"てくる"},{"t":" so often covers a beginning; the movement had to start somewhere behind you to have reached here."}]'::jsonb),
  ('60fc7d38eb067f50a78182c3e60b781105629282afda89b6be7ca7f9ad684888', '何か is the question word with the か that leaves things unsettled.', '[{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"か"},{"t":" is the question word with the "},{"t":"か"},{"t":" that leaves things unsettled."}]'::jsonb),
  ('5d117131f34c2c651d3a12d3006137d41829c6c3506597c70d5abca943428019', 'The か of a question, attached to 何 rather than to a sentence: the "what" is left open, so it names something without knowing it. 誰か, どこか and いつか are the same move.

With も instead, the range is swept rather than left open; 何も with a negative gives "nothing". か opens one slot; も takes them all.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"か"},{"t":" of a question, attached to "},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":" rather than to a sentence: the \"what\" is left open, so it names something without knowing it. "},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ"},{"t":"か"},{"t":", "},{"t":"どこか"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"いつか"},{"t":" are the same move.\n\nWith "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" instead, the range is swept rather than left open; "},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"も"},{"t":" with a negative gives \"nothing\". "},{"t":"か"},{"t":" opens one slot; "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" takes them all."}]'::jsonb),
  ('cbe3b175ac8620784647c403253ad748e4b6a0c0e1f95c2ba478b0b1ccfc876d', 'けれども is the adjective''s old 已然形 ending けれ with the concessive ども.', '[{"t":"けれども"},{"t":" is the adjective''s old "},{"t":"已然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" ending "},{"t":"けれ"},{"t":" with the concessive "},{"t":"ども"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0d874000ea67a37090cf67fc65cef857e64797633f9962333bda6b5db55e2288', 'The けれ is the one in 寒けれ, not the auxiliary けり. It joined ども, Old Japanese for "even though", at the end of the Muromachi period and then generalised to other predicates; けれど and けど follow in the Edo period.

That ども is the same particle inside といえども at N1. The length tracks the formality exactly: けれども is the whole phrase, けど is what survives casual speech, which is a measure of how much of it you bothered to say.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"けれ"},{"t":" is the one in "},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"けれ"},{"t":", not the auxiliary "},{"t":"けり"},{"t":". It joined "},{"t":"ども"},{"t":", Old Japanese for \"even though\", at the end of the Muromachi period and then generalised to other predicates; "},{"t":"けれど"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"けど"},{"t":" follow in the Edo period.\n\nThat "},{"t":"ども"},{"t":" is the same particle inside "},{"t":"といえども"},{"t":" at N1. The length tracks the formality exactly: "},{"t":"けれども"},{"t":" is the whole phrase, "},{"t":"けど"},{"t":" is what survives casual speech, which is a measure of how much of it you bothered to say."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a08c8751ea2f5090cb4d3fbffcd39ebdb632073b5d5e650a645a4862f5226158', 'The の is the listing の of 行くの行かないの, holding each item at arm''s length rather than saying it straight. 暑いだの寒いだの quotes the complaints instead of making them.

That distance is the irritation, and it need not be aimed outward: 掃除だの洗濯だので一日つぶれた grumbles about your own day. What だの will not do is sit in a neutral list where と or や would serve.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"の"},{"t":" is the listing "},{"t":"の"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"くの"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"かないの"},{"t":", holding each item at arm''s length rather than saying it straight. "},{"t":"暑","r":"あつ"},{"t":"いだの"},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"いだの"},{"t":" quotes the complaints instead of making them.\n\nThat distance is the irritation, and it need not be aimed outward: "},{"t":"掃除","r":"そうじ"},{"t":"だの"},{"t":"洗濯","r":"せんたく"},{"t":"だので"},{"t":"一日","r":"いちにち"},{"t":"つぶれた"},{"t":" grumbles about your own day. What "},{"t":"だの"},{"t":" will not do is sit in a neutral list where "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" or "},{"t":"や"},{"t":" would serve."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b1206d66f29f1fe6a74afc3b26cbbdf91ad104d555a74593b0ef5ac6ccf12b97', 'くらい is 位, a noun meaning rank or grade.', '[{"t":"くらい"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"位","r":"くらい"},{"t":", a noun meaning rank or grade."}]'::jsonb),
  ('74cbec3535d493bd38e5d5cf2db1fd61e90f3747d38c94d54dc45d0d62a29d7d', 'The 位 of 一位 and 位置: a place on a scale. Put after a quantity it gives the approximate reading, because naming a grade is vaguer than naming a number.

The belittling use is the same scale pointed downward. 掃除くらいしなさい says cleaning ranks that low, so there is no excuse: the dismissiveness is the noun doing its ordinary job.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"位","r":"くらい"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"一","r":"いっち"},{"t":"位","r":"くらい"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"位置","r":"いち"},{"t":": a place on a scale. Put after a quantity it gives the approximate reading, because naming a grade is vaguer than naming a number.\n\nThe belittling use is the same scale pointed downward. "},{"t":"掃除","r":"そうじ"},{"t":"くらいしなさい"},{"t":" says cleaning ranks that low, so there is no excuse: the dismissiveness is the noun doing its ordinary job."}]'::jsonb),
  ('8bc819e130693d44de37b87431e029cda3ba3d7392e1a3074d1e5820ce5c7c5b', 'ように言う reports an instruction as a manner rather than as words.', '[{"t":"ように"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"う"},{"t":" reports an instruction as a manner rather than as words."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0aa29a31a98c66ccb154e17273b521ab3d29fc46e85c66010b882c6f75a363f9', 'The 様 of ようだ with に and a verb of saying. What is quoted is not the sentence spoken but the way things were to be, which is why the verb in front stays plain and no quotation marks are implied.

It is how Japanese gives indirect commands without the imperative: 静かにするように言った reports that quiet was asked for, without repeating however bluntly it was put.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"様","r":"さま"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"ようだ"},{"t":" with "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" and a verb of saying. What is quoted is not the sentence spoken but the way things were to be, which is why the verb in front stays plain and no quotation marks are implied.\n\nIt is how Japanese gives indirect commands without the imperative: "},{"t":"静","r":"しず"},{"t":"かにするように"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"った"},{"t":" reports that quiet was asked for, without repeating however bluntly it was put."}]'::jsonb),
  ('eb823f53495de8c5bdcbacd06091de5258cb116b28fdc15fec2d6dee6aea6b91', 'The 連体形 was the classical form for standing before a noun, and when it merged with the sentence-ending form the modifying ability came along. 昨日買った本 is "yesterday-bought book" with no "that" or "which" anywhere.

