  ('dae78af6abab1fed46b30df49feaf39311ec8f029d32d36a5055ecc6101e2525', 'べき is the 連体形 of べし, the Old Japanese auxiliary of obligation and expectation.', '[{"t":"べき"},{"t":" is the "},{"t":"連","r":"つ"},{"t":"体","r":"たい"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"べし"},{"t":", the Old Japanese auxiliary of obligation and expectation."}]'::jsonb),
  ('aeade6e37f6574248e5bca4ab3fc95d0cb2d583b8ce198c77aff691a80367200', 'べし had a full set of forms, and べき is the one that stood before a noun. That is why the modern pattern is べき and never べし except in set phrases; you are using a form that was built to modify something.

