  ('5d76e16ce1b9c385e7d1aa5d4c52a780dbd2175b86aa82ddbd6f766dd08df312', '様 is an ordinary noun, the 様 of 様子 and of 皆様. 雨のようだ is literally "it has the appearance of rain".

Recognising it as a noun explains an entire family at once. ように is that noun with に, "in the manner that"; ようになる is coming to be that way; ようにする is making it that way; みたい is the casual stand-in for the same thing. Five patterns, one word, and it is why よう takes の after a noun: 学生のよう, exactly as any other noun would. みたい, worn down further, attaches directly instead: 学生みたい.', '[{"t":"様","r":"さま"},{"t":" is an ordinary noun, the "},{"t":"様","r":"さま"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"様子","r":"ようす"},{"t":" and of "},{"t":"皆様","r":"みなさま"},{"t":". "},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ"},{"t":"のようだ"},{"t":" is literally \"it has the appearance of rain\".\n\nRecognising it as a noun explains an entire family at once. "},{"t":"ように"},{"t":" is that noun with "},{"t":"に"},{"t":", \"in the manner that\"; "},{"t":"ようになる"},{"t":" is coming to be that way; "},{"t":"ようにする"},{"t":" is making it that way; "},{"t":"みたい"},{"t":" is the casual stand-in for the same thing. Five patterns, one word, and it is why "},{"t":"よう"},{"t":" takes "},{"t":"の"},{"t":" after a noun: "},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい"},{"t":"のよう"},{"t":", exactly as any other noun would. "},{"t":"みたい"},{"t":", worn down further, attaches directly instead: "},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい"},{"t":"みたい"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
