  ('2870a08204b2af13a274704729364ac475ac6474e1aeb40ebd7eb0515057edae', 'あげる is 上げる, "to raise", and the upward hand-off was originally deferential.', '[{"t":"あげる"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"上","r":"あ"},{"t":"げる"},{"t":", \"to raise\", and the upward hand-off was originally deferential."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b8834041c07d674d26bddfd9906b3b5dadc5301db080ea7f7c225844dc74ea6f', 'Japanese sorts giving by direction rather than by who owns what, and 上げる sends the thing up toward the recipient. That was politeness: あげる rose as the courteous replacement for やる, and handing something up puts the receiver above you, not below.

