  ('1799c2df2f334b97e454658b33e3f508b4e333f1b36e7451a8173c2f1a9feafc', 'The chain is けれども → けれど → けど. けれ is the old 已然形 ending of adjectives, the one in 寒けれ, and ども is an Old Japanese concessive meaning "even though"; the pair formed at the end of the Muromachi period and spread to other predicates, with けれど and けど following in Edo.

The length of the form tracks its formality precisely: けれども is the whole thing, けど is what survives casual speech. That is not an arbitrary register scale; it is literally how much of the original phrase you bothered to say.', '[{"t":"The chain is "},{"t":"けれども"},{"t":" → "},{"t":"けれど"},{"t":" → "},{"t":"けど"},{"t":". "},{"t":"けれ"},{"t":" is the old "},{"t":"已然"},{"t":"形","r":"なり"},{"t":" ending of adjectives, the one in "},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"けれ"},{"t":", and "},{"t":"ども"},{"t":" is an Old Japanese concessive meaning \"even though\"; the pair formed at the end of the Muromachi period and spread to other predicates, with "},{"t":"けれど"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"けど"},{"t":" following in Edo.\n\nThe length of the form tracks its formality precisely: "},{"t":"けれども"},{"t":" is the whole thing, "},{"t":"けど"},{"t":" is what survives casual speech. That is not an arbitrary register scale; it is literally how much of the original phrase you bothered to say."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3603d4f823ad2312f6163d4f82268e82b5b24e24c65e97dc9cfc355573a05fe2', 'げ is け, "air, appearance", conventionally written 気.', '[{"t":"げ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"け"},{"t":", \"air, appearance\", conventionally written "},{"t":"気","r":"げ"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d84e1e0d8d075185adddc1a00d59bc8a21fbd001ad751dca7062844cb1cd3258', 'Whether that け is the Chinese loan 気 or a native noun the character was fitted to is not settled. What it does is not in doubt: it reports an appearance, and 悲しげ is a sad air about someone. The げ-adjectives are characteristic of Heian prose: うつくしげ, 心細げ.

So げ, 気味 and そう all describe outward signs, differing in register and reach. げ is literary and works on emotions; 気味 is a trace, usually unwelcome; そう is the plain spoken one. All three exist because Japanese will not let you assert another person''s feelings flatly.', '[{"t":"Whether that "},{"t":"け"},{"t":" is the Chinese loan "},{"t":"気","r":"げ"},{"t":" or a native noun the character was fitted to is not settled. What it does is not in doubt: it reports an appearance, and "},{"t":"悲","r":"かな"},{"t":"しげ"},{"t":" is a sad air about someone. The "},{"t":"げ"},{"t":"-adjectives are characteristic of Heian prose: "},{"t":"うつくしげ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"心","r":"しん"},{"t":"細","r":"ささ"},{"t":"げ"},{"t":".\n\nSo "},{"t":"げ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"気味","r":"ぎみ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"そう"},{"t":" all describe outward signs, differing in register and reach. "},{"t":"げ"},{"t":" is literary and works on emotions; "},{"t":"気味","r":"ぎみ"},{"t":" is a trace, usually unwelcome; "},{"t":"そう"},{"t":" is the plain spoken one. All three exist because Japanese will not let you assert another person''s feelings flatly."}]'::jsonb),
  ('21a2d506ec41d0b541b5e132900cf2d1f561136bc6bef25f0577906914c8c71c', 'において is 置く, the same verb as 〜ておく at N4.', '[{"t":"において"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"置","r":"お"},{"t":"く"},{"t":", the same verb as 〜"},{"t":"ておく"},{"t":" at N4."}]'::jsonb),
  ('607d758febebaec172ee72b0f33d41f8a85b7c4ce0efcd1929df4f10b22a3b80', '置く is to put something down and leave it. において sets the matter down in a place and discusses it there, a job it grew into through kanbun reading, where 於 was rendered with this verb, which is how a verb of placing came to do locative work at all.

So the formality is not in the meaning but in the register: において is the written language''s で, and it is doing the same locating job with a heavier verb. That it shares a root with the everyday 〜ておく is easy to miss because one is written in kanji and the other never is.', '[{"t":"置","r":"お"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is to put something down and leave it. "},{"t":"において"},{"t":" sets the matter down in a place and discusses it there, a job it grew into through kanbun reading, where "},{"t":"於","r":"お"},{"t":" was rendered with this verb, which is how a verb of placing came to do locative work at all.\n\nSo the formality is not in the meaning but in the register: "},{"t":"において"},{"t":" is the written language''s "},{"t":"で"},{"t":", and it is doing the same locating job with a heavier verb. That it shares a root with the everyday 〜"},{"t":"ておく"},{"t":" is easy to miss because one is written in kanji and the other never is."}]'::jsonb),
  ('df47a22767956f3f7084555f6016b1fa6693b9114051e1a4a39a5a8a92f747b8', 'The stem is separated from its own する and も slips into the gap: 見もしない, 返事もしない. The も is the one that means "even".

That splitting is what makes it emphatic. An ordinary negative denies the act; this one denies that the act was so much as attempted, which is why it carries reproach.', '[{"t":"The stem is separated from its own "},{"t":"する"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" slips into the gap: "},{"t":"見","r":"み"},{"t":"もしない"},{"t":", "},{"t":"返事","r":"へんじ"},{"t":"もしない"},{"t":". The "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" is the one that means \"even\".\n\nThat splitting is what makes it emphatic. An ordinary negative denies the act; this one denies that the act was so much as attempted, which is why it carries reproach."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a0f9a28a498fc1d5f61bf6c2dd4268f81d5efce8e8f6a9c0cf1c17c8f977bb82', 'The nominaliser from N4 and the adverbial form of なし. 休むことなく働いた names resting as a matter and says there was none of it.

It is the written counterpart of ないで, and the difference is exactly that ことなく makes the absent thing a noun first. That is why it takes only verbs describing a whole activity, and why it sounds wrong in speech.', '[{"t":"The nominaliser from N4 and the adverbial form of "},{"t":"なし"},{"t":". "},{"t":"休","r":"やす"},{"t":"むことなく"},{"t":"働","r":"はたら"},{"t":"いた"},{"t":" names resting as a matter and says there was none of it.\n\nIt is the written counterpart of "},{"t":"ないで"},{"t":", and the difference is exactly that "},{"t":"ことなく"},{"t":" makes the absent thing a noun first. That is why it takes only verbs describing a whole activity, and why it sounds wrong in speech."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3f9e486e100b2c4ec8962e9f19a68830126d8aaaa4906d8bd5cbad279db336db', 'なる is the verb of things happening of themselves. Put a matter into it and the matter settles without anyone being said to have settled it.

That is why it is the polite way to report a decision you did in fact make: 来月転勤することになりました leaves the deciding unattributed, which is more comfortable than announcing it. The construction is not passive, but it does the work a passive would do in English.', '[{"t":"なる"},{"t":" is the verb of things happening of themselves. Put a matter into it and the matter settles without anyone being said to have settled it.\n\nThat is why it is the polite way to report a decision you did in fact make: "},{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ"},{"t":"転勤","r":"てんきん"},{"t":"することになりました"},{"t":" leaves the deciding unattributed, which is more comfortable than announcing it. The construction is not passive, but it does the work a passive would do in English."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e5a5e4219afe7e1bc101c42692fea110bf44958508653743dd5b337d0f4575f0', 'さる is 然る ("such, that being so"), the same 然 as in そう and 然し.', '[{"t":"さる"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"然る"},{"t":" (\"such, that being so\"), the same "},{"t":"然"},{"t":" as in "},{"t":"そう"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"然し"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('7b3bc5a6e58688dd35f9e54da7e83615eaf65ef9825578cc03634af433a236a0', '然る is a classical modifier meaning of that kind. さることながら is "that being such a matter, and yet".

So the pattern grants the first thing fully before raising the second, which is why it cannot be used to dismiss. 味もさることながらサービスがいい praises the food and then says the service is more remarkable still.', '[{"t":"然る"},{"t":" is a classical modifier meaning of that kind. "},{"t":"さることながら"},{"t":" is \"that being such a matter, and yet\".\n\nSo the pattern grants the first thing fully before raising the second, which is why it cannot be used to dismiss. "},{"t":"味","r":"あじ"},{"t":"もさることながらサービスがいい"},{"t":" praises the food and then says the service is more remarkable still."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0eb51202ed95ebc6f70af413b585c38fe3206a96e46ae5ef0a89d10fa9a68c07', 'しまう is 仕舞う, to put away, to close up a shop for the night.', '[{"t":"しまう"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"仕","r":"つか"},{"t":"舞","r":"ま"},{"t":"う"},{"t":", to put away, to close up a shop for the night."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b816e8efa8b0f0876be93876a9fd7c4baad14b2cfad63ca3a98c7f5a93526480', 'The literal verb is still in use: 片付けて仕舞う, to tidy things away. It carries finishing and shutting in one action.

Both senses of the compound come straight out of that, and they are not two meanings but one. 食べてしまった is finished off completely; 忘れてしまった is regrettable, because a thing that has been put away and the shutters brought down is a thing you cannot get back at. The casual 〜ちゃう is a contraction of 〜てしまう, and 〜じゃう of 〜でしまう.', '[{"t":"The literal verb is still in use: "},{"t":"片付","r":"かたづ"},{"t":"けて"},{"t":"仕","r":"つか"},{"t":"舞","r":"ま"},{"t":"う"},{"t":", to tidy things away. It carries finishing and shutting in one action.\n\nBoth senses of the compound come straight out of that, and they are not two meanings but one. "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べてしまった"},{"t":" is finished off completely; "},{"t":"忘","r":"わす"},{"t":"れてしまった"},{"t":" is regrettable, because a thing that has been put away and the shutters brought down is a thing you cannot get back at. The casual 〜"},{"t":"ちゃう"},{"t":" is a contraction of 〜"},{"t":"てしまう"},{"t":", and 〜"},{"t":"じゃう"},{"t":" of 〜"},{"t":"でしまう"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('543f16f4cb099c00abe1ad4535482f7c762e8f95fe7f35a4d2ffea273c83bf49', 'The same fossil-imperative trick as であれ. 行くにしろ行かないにしろ commands each possibility to stand and then says neither matters.

にせよ is the same verb in its classical imperative, which is why the two are interchangeable and にせよ is the stiffer. Japanese builds concession out of commands more than once, and this is the clearest case.', '[{"t":"The same fossil-imperative trick as "},{"t":"であれ"},{"t":". "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"くにしろ"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"かないにしろ"},{"t":" commands each possibility to stand and then says neither matters.\n\n"},{"t":"にせよ"},{"t":" is the same verb in its classical imperative, which is why the two are interchangeable and "},{"t":"にせよ"},{"t":" is the stiffer. Japanese builds concession out of commands more than once, and this is the clearest case."}]'::jsonb),
  ('2b9f3c4fdf1fca20ea0ff287d2be561db2182a9046f09a47bfd8a2f0c79aa352', 'すぎない is 過ぎる negated: it does not go beyond.', '[{"t":"すぎない"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"過","r":"よ"},{"t":"ぎる"},{"t":" negated: it does not go beyond."}]'::jsonb),
  ('2951248bee83dbcd4821d5b6267b277a8c6078e5de87173c6aaedf8f629e6923', 'The same 過ぎる taught at N5 in 食べすぎる, where passing the proper point is a complaint. Negated it becomes a limit: this does not pass that point.

So にすぎない always belittles. 学生にすぎない is "no more than a student", and the dismissiveness is the verb refusing to let the thing exceed its bound.', '[{"t":"The same "},{"t":"過","r":"よ"},{"t":"ぎる"},{"t":" taught at N5 in "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べすぎる"},{"t":", where passing the proper point is a complaint. Negated it becomes a limit: this does not pass that point.\n\nSo "},{"t":"にすぎない"},{"t":" always belittles. "},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい"},{"t":"にすぎない"},{"t":" is \"no more than a student\", and the dismissiveness is the verb refusing to let the thing exceed its bound."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0a82ec2f991288ac65877ed66c85a97e9d45de0dc560750ba6a7c97104a45e85', 'すみません is 済む negated: "this is not settled".', '[{"t":"すみません"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"済","r":"す"},{"t":"む"},{"t":" negated: \"this is not settled\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('101cf38b655b059a64aa0220e8119b76d824c7e2a76d2820f5045389fe6debc4', '済む means to be finished or squared away. すみません says the matter is not concluded: something remains owed.

Which is why one word covers apology, attention-getting and thanks. All three are the same admission that a debt is outstanding, and it explains why すみません can be a warmer thank-you than ありがとう; it acknowledges the trouble rather than only the result. The formal 済みません is the same word spelled out.', '[{"t":"済","r":"す"},{"t":"む"},{"t":" means to be finished or squared away. "},{"t":"すみません"},{"t":" says the matter is not concluded: something remains owed.\n\nWhich is why one word covers apology, attention-getting and thanks. All three are the same admission that a debt is outstanding, and it explains why "},{"t":"すみません"},{"t":" can be a warmer thank-you than "},{"t":"ありがとう"},{"t":"; it acknowledges the trouble rather than only the result. The formal "},{"t":"済","r":"す"},{"t":"みません"},{"t":" is the same word spelled out."}]'::jsonb),
  ('5e9772ba4a94b63014b2d3660226c94f6dd5dbeeeb93d1579872dc1878d8ae08', 'ずくめ has a contested origin: 尽く and 竦める both have backers.', '[{"t":"ずくめ"},{"t":" has a contested origin: "},{"t":"尽","r":"つ"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"竦める"},{"t":" both have backers."}]'::jsonb),
  ('ba8149c3717bd9066b3ccd9430ca4af548c15a28aed2b0063ca811dd723985a0', 'The kana is the clue. It is written ずくめ and not づくめ, which is itself a sign that lexicographers do not read it as a transparent 尽く; 三省堂国語辞典 derives it instead from 竦める, "to bind tight". Either way the sense is a thing filled out with one quality and no remainder.

Which is why it takes only a small set of nouns and describes a totality rather than a mess: 黒ずくめ, いいことずくめ, and 規則ずくめ (that last one showing the totality can oppress rather than please). だらけ is a scattering of something; ずくめ leaves no room for anything else.', '[{"t":"The kana is the clue. It is written "},{"t":"ずくめ"},{"t":" and not "},{"t":"づくめ"},{"t":", which is itself a sign that lexicographers do not read it as a transparent "},{"t":"尽","r":"つ"},{"t":"く"},{"t":"; "},{"t":"三","r":"さん"},{"t":"省","r":"はぶ"},{"t":"堂"},{"t":"国語","r":"こくご"},{"t":"辞典","r":"じてん"},{"t":" derives it instead from "},{"t":"竦める"},{"t":", \"to bind tight\". Either way the sense is a thing filled out with one quality and no remainder.\n\nWhich is why it takes only a small set of nouns and describes a totality rather than a mess: "},{"t":"黒","r":"くろ"},{"t":"ずくめ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"いいことずくめ"},{"t":", and "},{"t":"規則","r":"きそく"},{"t":"ずくめ"},{"t":" (that last one showing the totality can oppress rather than please). "},{"t":"だらけ"},{"t":" is a scattering of something; "},{"t":"ずくめ"},{"t":" leaves no room for anything else."}]'::jsonb),
  ('23056996220efc721bd7e85c75576ea136efc8162ec5ed146ea2686c6717c836', 'せい is 所為, literally "the thing done", a doing that something is charged with.', '[{"t":"せい"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"所為","r":"しょい"},{"t":", literally \"the thing done\", a doing that something is charged with."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f9b57a04c91d4791045f817433bbc11855268d37d14d756d0b4672c4967bcb61', 'The そう that attaches to a stem and reports how something looks, with も adding "even" and ない denying it. 雨が降りそうもない says it does not even have the appearance.

The も is what makes it stronger than そうではない. Denying that something even looks likely rules it out further than denying that it is.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"そう"},{"t":" that attaches to a stem and reports how something looks, with "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" adding \"even\" and "},{"t":"ない"},{"t":" denying it. "},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降","r":"お"},{"t":"りそうもない"},{"t":" says it does not even have the appearance.\n\nThe "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" is what makes it stronger than "},{"t":"そうではない"},{"t":". Denying that something even looks likely rules it out further than denying that it is."}]'::jsonb),
  ('98dde92fb739a84a369345490208aa6e7876c5b6cafba97fd317b96322cbf608', 'そこねる is 損ねる, from 損なう: "to damage, to spoil".', '[{"t":"そこねる"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"損","r":"そん"},{"t":"ねる"},{"t":", from "},{"t":"損","r":"そこ"},{"t":"なう"},{"t":": \"to damage, to spoil\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f2006d32625861f4d67b39ce2b1dae3d0ee368271dbf93d04a403b9383dad3c4', '機嫌を損ねる is putting someone out of temper. As a suffix it spoils the action itself.

