  ('2867ce0cbcba8030daf96a4ddd52eed741ce13d70d3bfa9d03f6f7e7c41e7732', 'たところで is the 所 of N4 with で: at that point, and no further.', '[{"t":"たところで"},{"t":" is the "},{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":" of N4 with "},{"t":"で"},{"t":": at that point, and no further."}]'::jsonb),
  ('579fca23240e2c4ae5c3f58a63d58e794aca4357211fa8cc13f6cf47fbf7247a', '所 locates a moment. The past tense puts it after the act, and で marks it as where matters stand.

So 謝ったところで許されない pictures the apology as already made inside a supposition: even granting it done, nothing follows. The た is perfective inside an irrealis rather than a report of something that happened, which is why 今から謝ったところで works. What comes after must be negative in import, though 無駄だ and 同じことだ serve as well as ない.', '[{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":" locates a moment. The past tense puts it after the act, and "},{"t":"で"},{"t":" marks it as where matters stand.\n\nSo "},{"t":"謝","r":"あやま"},{"t":"ったところで"},{"t":"許","r":"ゆる"},{"t":"されない"},{"t":" pictures the apology as already made inside a supposition: even granting it done, nothing follows. The "},{"t":"た"},{"t":" is perfective inside an irrealis rather than a report of something that happened, which is why "},{"t":"今","r":"こん"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"謝","r":"あやま"},{"t":"ったところで"},{"t":" works. What comes after must be negative in import, though "},{"t":"無駄","r":"むだ"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"同","r":"おな"},{"t":"じことだ"},{"t":" serve as well as "},{"t":"ない"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('92bad21e5e70a4fe8ae6d9cf647898100be9eb43ebdfdfe04ee5d9dd47bf2f3a', 'たび is 度, an occasion, the same 度 counted in 一度 and 何度.', '[{"t":"たび"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"度","r":"たび"},{"t":", an occasion, the same "},{"t":"度","r":"たび"},{"t":" counted in "},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"何度","r":"なんど"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f702e3bd6f69341a0d0e0dccc5b8f5a257a5d777e7476b5fccc3d10f29e2f836', 'A noun meaning a time or an instance. 会うたびに is "on each occasion of meeting", with no idiom involved.

Because it is a noun it takes what all nouns take: a plain-form clause in front, 会うたびに, or another noun with の, 旅行のたびに. Being a counter has nothing to do with it; うち, まま and かぎり behave the same way.', '[{"t":"A noun meaning a time or an instance. "},{"t":"会","r":"あ"},{"t":"うたびに"},{"t":" is \"on each occasion of meeting\", with no idiom involved.\n\nBecause it is a noun it takes what all nouns take: a plain-form clause in front, "},{"t":"会","r":"あ"},{"t":"うたびに"},{"t":", or another noun with "},{"t":"の"},{"t":", "},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう"},{"t":"のたびに"},{"t":". Being a counter has nothing to do with it; "},{"t":"うち"},{"t":", "},{"t":"まま"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"かぎり"},{"t":" behave the same way."}]'::jsonb),
  ('91565d378aae247c5d710f03524556dd54069c1962997d408de9bdfed6638e54', 'たまらない is 堪る negated: one cannot bear it.', '[{"t":"たまらない"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"堪","r":"こら"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" negated: one cannot bear it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('fd9ae787f6296f1330e3721c0ca5f1ed7d06efc6fd01d02769759324d72cebd1', '堪る is an ordinary verb of enduring, close kin to 耐える. 暑くてたまらない is heat that cannot be borne.

Because the verb is about the speaker''s own endurance, the pattern only works on your own sensations and feelings. Another person''s unbearable heat needs たまらないらしい or 暑がっている, the same restriction that gives がる its job at N4.', '[{"t":"堪","r":"こら"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" is an ordinary verb of enduring, close kin to "},{"t":"耐","r":"た"},{"t":"える"},{"t":". "},{"t":"暑","r":"あつ"},{"t":"くてたまらない"},{"t":" is heat that cannot be borne.\n\nBecause the verb is about the speaker''s own endurance, the pattern only works on your own sensations and feelings. Another person''s unbearable heat needs "},{"t":"たまらないらしい"},{"t":" or "},{"t":"暑","r":"あつ"},{"t":"がっている"},{"t":", the same restriction that gives "},{"t":"がる"},{"t":" its job at N4."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0d0a9b6b5b622ca3e88e1e0c3d6c7a3f506aaa174c7de25c34ba901e0d9d39ba', 'ため is the noun 為 again, the same one that gives purpose.', '[{"t":"ため"},{"t":" is the noun "},{"t":"為","r":"す"},{"t":" again, the same one that gives purpose."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4f60732bc8aff4169b56e08d867e2da877637a1e665e1de7abf202193fb53d1d', 'It is a native noun meaning a benefit or an account, with 為 as its spelling. Pointed forward it gives purpose, 勉強するために; pointed backward it gives cause, 雨のために中止.

One noun, and which reading you get depends on whether what follows was aimed at or merely resulted. That is why the cause use belongs to written notices, where から and ので would be too conversational.', '[{"t":"It is a native noun meaning a benefit or an account, with "},{"t":"為","r":"す"},{"t":" as its spelling. Pointed forward it gives purpose, "},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう"},{"t":"するために"},{"t":"; pointed backward it gives cause, "},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ"},{"t":"のために"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし"},{"t":".\n\nOne noun, and which reading you get depends on whether what follows was aimed at or merely resulted. That is why the cause use belongs to written notices, where "},{"t":"から"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"ので"},{"t":" would be too conversational."}]'::jsonb),
  ('29690fad2cde105640939a9c016e1355d583ada9864e58322283005e7110851a', 'Classical たり asserted identity, as なり did. とも is the concessive "even if". 一円たりとも is "even were it one yen".

That is why the pattern demands a number and a negative afterwards: it concedes the smallest possible case and then refuses even that. 一日たりとも忘れない.', '[{"t":"Classical "},{"t":"たり"},{"t":" asserted identity, as "},{"t":"なり"},{"t":" did. "},{"t":"とも"},{"t":" is the concessive \"even if\". "},{"t":"一","r":"いっち"},{"t":"円","r":"えん"},{"t":"たりとも"},{"t":" is \"even were it one yen\".\n\nThat is why the pattern demands a number and a negative afterwards: it concedes the smallest possible case and then refuses even that. "},{"t":"一日","r":"いちにち"},{"t":"たりとも"},{"t":"忘","r":"わす"},{"t":"れない"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d5768835457279c4cddbb1c85f7408b4573a2bc771b566e3a766f2510967e146', 'だけ comes from the noun 丈, a height or limit. でなく denies that the limit falls there, which opens the sentence up for whatever follows.

That is why a second clause is obligatory and usually carries も: the construction has denied a boundary and something has to come through the gap.', '[{"t":"だけ"},{"t":" comes from the noun "},{"t":"丈","r":"だけ"},{"t":", a height or limit. "},{"t":"でなく"},{"t":" denies that the limit falls there, which opens the sentence up for whatever follows.\n\nThat is why a second clause is obligatory and usually carries "},{"t":"も"},{"t":": the construction has denied a boundary and something has to come through the gap."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f82ee8a1e4006df8c66019ceb2ee91c403b404e197bcebab22b96a69cfe15129', 'であらう contracted to だろう. The polite でしょう is built on です instead, でせう before the kana reform, so the two are parallel formations on different copulas rather than one derived from the other.

The final う is the same volitional that gives 行こう, which is why だろう guesses rather than states. The volitional was never about certainty; it marks the speaker projecting rather than reporting.', '[{"t":"であらう"},{"t":" contracted to "},{"t":"だろう"},{"t":". The polite "},{"t":"でしょう"},{"t":" is built on "},{"t":"です"},{"t":" instead, "},{"t":"でせう"},{"t":" before the kana reform, so the two are parallel formations on different copulas rather than one derived from the other.\n\nThe final "},{"t":"う"},{"t":" is the same volitional that gives "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"こう"},{"t":", which is why "},{"t":"だろう"},{"t":" guesses rather than states. The volitional was never about certainty; it marks the speaker projecting rather than reporting."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a2875e93fd0f0fe3121c942489c7a3a6c7b6722f981a8196bdc68a3653ad61a4', 'ついで is つぎて, from 次ぐ "to come next", the thing that follows on.', '[{"t":"ついで"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"つぎて"},{"t":", from "},{"t":"次","r":"つ"},{"t":"ぐ"},{"t":" \"to come next\", the thing that follows on."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3fbb7c13212bf1527daa56ef43c5beafcad3ec867609fc40825525eb40c0fb60', 'A regular sound change, つぎて to ついで. 序 is a kanji fitted to the word afterwards; the Sino-Japanese morpheme is not where it comes from.

What follows on has to follow something, which is why the pattern needs a main errand to attach to. 買い物のついでに寄る works because the shopping was going to happen anyway; the second act rides on the first rather than standing alone.', '[{"t":"A regular sound change, "},{"t":"つぎて"},{"t":" to "},{"t":"ついで"},{"t":". "},{"t":"序"},{"t":" is a kanji fitted to the word afterwards; the Sino-Japanese morpheme is not where it comes from.\n\nWhat follows on has to follow something, which is why the pattern needs a main errand to attach to. "},{"t":"買","r":"か"},{"t":"い"},{"t":"物","r":"もの"},{"t":"のついでに"},{"t":"寄","r":"よ"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" works because the shopping was going to happen anyway; the second act rides on the first rather than standing alone."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c28d90de2cc6b74244e7711e6eb3d7bddb29504bfd8f65f70998227544b7b10d', 'いる is the ordinary verb "to exist (animate)". So 食べている is "exists in the state of having eaten/eating".

Once you see it as te-form + exist, the two meanings stop being arbitrary. If the verb describes an activity, existing-in-that-activity means it is ongoing. If the verb describes an instantaneous change, existing-after-that-change means you are in the resulting state. Same construction; the verb decides which reading makes sense.', '[{"t":"いる"},{"t":" is the ordinary verb \"to exist (animate)\". So "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べている"},{"t":" is \"exists in the state of having eaten/eating\".\n\nOnce you see it as te-form + exist, the two meanings stop being arbitrary. If the verb describes an activity, existing-in-that-activity means it is ongoing. If the verb describes an instantaneous change, existing-after-that-change means you are in the resulting state. Same construction; the verb decides which reading makes sense."}]'::jsonb),
  ('ca1959b897a88025d085632c0d44ede39ffbc63d7b246afcd4c322179953deec', 'から marks a starting point, in space, time or reason. Put it after a te-form and the completed action becomes the point everything after starts from.

That is the difference from a bare te-form chain. 食べて出かけた merely lists two acts in order; 食べてから出かけた insists the first was finished before the second began.', '[{"t":"から"},{"t":" marks a starting point, in space, time or reason. Put it after a te-form and the completed action becomes the point everything after starts from.\n\nThat is the difference from a bare te-form chain. "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べて"},{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけた"},{"t":" merely lists two acts in order; "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べてから"},{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけた"},{"t":" insists the first was finished before the second began."}]'::jsonb),
  ('af369bb9ab1f08ec68f1a78045f26cc26efd66feac807ad8f37e38a1a95eb2b2', 'てばかりいる is the 計り of N3, a measure, applied to how someone spends their time.', '[{"t":"てばかりいる"},{"t":" is the "},{"t":"計","r":"けい"},{"t":"り"},{"t":" of N3, a measure, applied to how someone spends their time."}]'::jsonb),
  ('03fb0e1809ff5c55acbd216acb4000cef688c1bcd27db2e21bfb091307685a91', 'ばかり is 計り, from 計る, "to measure": an amount, narrowed to "that amount and no more". With a te-form and いる it measures the whole of someone''s activity as one thing.

遊んでばかりいる is nothing but playing, and the いる matters; it makes it a state they are in rather than an act they performed, which is where the disapproval sits.', '[{"t":"ばかり"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"計","r":"けい"},{"t":"り"},{"t":", from "},{"t":"計","r":"はか"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", \"to measure\": an amount, narrowed to \"that amount and no more\". With a te-form and "},{"t":"いる"},{"t":" it measures the whole of someone''s activity as one thing.\n\n"},{"t":"遊","r":"あそ"},{"t":"んでばかりいる"},{"t":" is nothing but playing, and the "},{"t":"いる"},{"t":" matters; it makes it a state they are in rather than an act they performed, which is where the disapproval sits."}]'::jsonb),
  ('126d7da840f4d75ed4a277cb05936277712c678cfb855c3f7ff0bd20b9ffd099', 'まで marks a limit. 断られたら諦めるまでだ says the giving up is where the matter stops, with nothing further to it.

The flatness is the particle. It is not resolve or defiance but a boundary drawn: that is as far as this goes. までのことだ is the same thing with こと inserted to make it a stated matter.', '[{"t":"まで"},{"t":" marks a limit. "},{"t":"断","r":"た"},{"t":"られたら"},{"t":"諦","r":"あきら"},{"t":"めるまでだ"},{"t":" says the giving up is where the matter stops, with nothing further to it.\n\nThe flatness is the particle. It is not resolve or defiance but a boundary drawn: that is as far as this goes. "},{"t":"までのことだ"},{"t":" is the same thing with "},{"t":"こと"},{"t":" inserted to make it a stated matter."}]'::jsonb),
  ('aba0453fd418465978abe2db9fcf43523c789a67b07468cd10966eead38012f2', 'にて is still written in formal notices. The に named a point and a linking て carried it onward; the pair contracted into で by the Muromachi period and was normal by Edo.

That single origin explains the four separate で lessons. Place of action, means, material and cause are all one particle naming the circumstance an action is carried out in. 公園で遊ぶ, ペンで書く, 木で作る, 風邪で休む: the English needs four prepositions and the Japanese never split.', '[{"t":"にて"},{"t":" is still written in formal notices. The "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" named a point and a linking "},{"t":"て"},{"t":" carried it onward; the pair contracted into "},{"t":"で"},{"t":" by the Muromachi period and was normal by Edo.\n\nThat single origin explains the four separate "},{"t":"で"},{"t":" lessons. Place of action, means, material and cause are all one particle naming the circumstance an action is carried out in. "},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊","r":"あそ"},{"t":"ぶ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"ペンで"},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":"く"},{"t":", "},{"t":"木","r":"き"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"作","r":"つく"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", "},{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"休","r":"やす"},{"t":"む"},{"t":": the English needs four prepositions and the Japanese never split."}]'::jsonb),
  ('5d5263f15b5480e1a19360299c2b23ba85321def2f6a9b7665adc26ed9196a53', 'あれ is the classical imperative of あり. Telling a hypothetical to be whatever it likes is a way of saying it changes nothing.

子供であれ大人であれ: be it a child, be it an adult. English does the same with "be it", a fossil imperative in both languages, doing concession rather than command.', '[{"t":"あれ"},{"t":" is the classical imperative of "},{"t":"あり"},{"t":". Telling a hypothetical to be whatever it likes is a way of saying it changes nothing.\n\n"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども"},{"t":"であれ"},{"t":"大人","r":"うし"},{"t":"であれ"},{"t":": be it a child, be it an adult. English does the same with \"be it\", a fossil imperative in both languages, doing concession rather than command."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c575fb16a34c6f3f5a690d50d4c70dbaa9112cd62b7c132a393dc4c9c7be32c5', '本と鉛筆 pairs two nouns exhaustively. 友達と行く pairs you with a companion. 「はい」と言った pairs an utterance with the saying of it, and that quotative use does come straight from this particle.

The conditional と of N4 is a separate thread (it is usually traced through とも rather than directly from the pairing use), so the family is not quite as tidy as it looks. What holds across all of these is that と joins two things and claims they belong together, which is why と lists completely where や lists loosely.', '[{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"鉛筆","r":"えんぴつ"},{"t":" pairs two nouns exhaustively. "},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" pairs you with a companion. 「"},{"t":"はい"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"った"},{"t":" pairs an utterance with the saying of it, and that quotative use does come straight from this particle.\n\nThe conditional "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" of N4 is a separate thread (it is usually traced through "},{"t":"とも"},{"t":" rather than directly from the pairing use), so the family is not quite as tidy as it looks. What holds across all of these is that "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" joins two things and claims they belong together, which is why "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" lists completely where "},{"t":"や"},{"t":" lists loosely."}]'::jsonb),
  ('27cb209892df606058732a1117e5c7d18f4ae89e9e580f53a9db100256a43360', 'といっても is the quotative と with 言う and も: "even saying that".', '[{"t":"といっても"},{"t":" is the quotative "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" with "},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"う"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"も"},{"t":": \"even saying that\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d7495665bd39299f324dcedec9abee609bdd23a31ad5a85a1093f8b5f2ab55fc', 'The whole N2 family of と言う compounds is built the same way, and reading them literally sorts them out. といっても concedes what was said and then cuts it down: 広いといっても、六畳だ.

See also からといって (just because it is said), とはいえ (that said), とは限らない (it is not limited to what is said). Four patterns, one verb of saying, each with a different particle doing the work.', '[{"t":"The whole N2 family of "},{"t":"と"},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"う"},{"t":" compounds is built the same way, and reading them literally sorts them out. "},{"t":"といっても"},{"t":" concedes what was said and then cuts it down: "},{"t":"広","r":"ひろ"},{"t":"いといっても"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"六","r":"りゅー"},{"t":"畳","r":"たとう"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":".\n\nSee also "},{"t":"からといって"},{"t":" (just because it is said), "},{"t":"とはいえ"},{"t":" (that said), "},{"t":"とは"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"らない"},{"t":" (it is not limited to what is said). Four patterns, one verb of saying, each with a different particle doing the work."}]'::jsonb),
  ('bf8b04b17a6a986fdea10d882590a50b4066fe328592262f9b561c7d6af52f3e', 'とおり is 通り, from 通る: "to pass along".', '[{"t":"とおり"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"通","r":"とお"},{"t":"り"},{"t":", from "},{"t":"通","r":"とお"},{"t":"る"},{"t":": \"to pass along\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a06fedcf584f937e1639d2721eb3840994530b456f18b85c9b0e35735b128874', 'A 通り is a street, a route, a way through. 言ったとおりに is going along the path the words laid down.

Because it is a noun it takes の after another noun (予定のとおり) and voices to どおり when it is fixed to one: 予定どおり. That voicing is rendaku, the habit many native nouns show in second position, a tendency of compounds rather than a rule, since plenty of nouns resist it.', '[{"t":"A "},{"t":"通","r":"とお"},{"t":"り"},{"t":" is a street, a route, a way through. "},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"ったとおりに"},{"t":" is going along the path the words laid down.\n\nBecause it is a noun it takes "},{"t":"の"},{"t":" after another noun ("},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい"},{"t":"のとおり"},{"t":") and voices to "},{"t":"どおり"},{"t":" when it is fixed to one: "},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい"},{"t":"どおり"},{"t":". That voicing is rendaku, the habit many native nouns show in second position, a tendency of compounds rather than a rule, since plenty of nouns resist it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('33eb9df24d5b79e3a899a791fc3b94f89de943c26785c7450a4701783fe04408', 'とき is 時, an ordinary noun, which is why a whole clause can sit in front of it.', '[{"t":"とき"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"時","r":"とき"},{"t":", an ordinary noun, which is why a whole clause can sit in front of it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('675a64a9fdbf983ff4f6ef3fed603aca989548d834781e17902dca85c0f64f47', '子供のとき and 東京に行くとき are the same construction as 赤い本: something describing a noun, with nothing added to join them.

