  ('fcc07707e66f49dec2505619f633f870a99ad1b0e8a49617c14c5768852cb817', 'ところ is 所, a place, Japanese locating a moment in time as a spot.', '[{"t":"ところ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":", a place, Japanese locating a moment in time as a spot."}]'::jsonb),
  ('cc5f0e81fceca77c5bd3b087599f3562cc9cfb2198a56b09708a83dd374834a4', '出かけるところ is the place in events where leaving is about to occur. The metaphor is so ordinary in English too ("at the point of leaving") that it usually passes unnoticed.

Seeing it as a place explains why the tense in front does all the work and ところ itself never changes: 出かけるところ is the spot before, 出かけているところ the spot during, 出かけたところ the spot just after. It also explains the particle, since ところ is a noun and takes を, に and で like any other.', '[{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけるところ"},{"t":" is the place in events where leaving is about to occur. The metaphor is so ordinary in English too (\"at the point of leaving\") that it usually passes unnoticed.\n\nSeeing it as a place explains why the tense in front does all the work and "},{"t":"ところ"},{"t":" itself never changes: "},{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけるところ"},{"t":" is the spot before, "},{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけているところ"},{"t":" the spot during, "},{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけたところ"},{"t":" the spot just after. It also explains the particle, since "},{"t":"ところ"},{"t":" is a noun and takes "},{"t":"を"},{"t":", "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"で"},{"t":" like any other."}]'::jsonb),
  ('fa618c9b11822a33463c4805bfdfc5d353030564024e2605748f2d0ecb485ef1', 'ところを is the 所 of N4 with を: the moment is the thing being cut into.', '[{"t":"ところを"},{"t":" is the "},{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":" of N4 with "},{"t":"を"},{"t":": the moment is the thing being cut into."}]'::jsonb),
  ('8797e1989c36ca22b1413a4e3adbb89ad977d596cb59b01500a027e2ee412afa', '所 locates a moment as a place. The を marks that moment as what the speaker''s intrusion acts upon.

お忙しいところをすみません apologises for the busy moment itself, not for anything done in it. That is why the pattern lives in apology and thanks (it names someone''s time as a thing you have taken), and equally in being caught or rescued mid-moment: 寝ているところを起こされた is the same intrusion from the other side.', '[{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":" locates a moment as a place. The "},{"t":"を"},{"t":" marks that moment as what the speaker''s intrusion acts upon.\n\n"},{"t":"お"},{"t":"忙","r":"いそが"},{"t":"しいところをすみません"},{"t":" apologises for the busy moment itself, not for anything done in it. That is why the pattern lives in apology and thanks (it names someone''s time as a thing you have taken), and equally in being caught or rescued mid-moment: "},{"t":"寝","r":"ね"},{"t":"ているところを"},{"t":"起","r":"お"},{"t":"こされた"},{"t":" is the same intrusion from the other side."}]'::jsonb),
  ('22cacbfd30c68c9d0245f80c0c2e807eddd0f05cf6d63c2feded04ea3e430282', 'The と that quotes, and する doing what it does in ことにする: making it so. 医者として来た is coming, taken as a doctor.

So it is not a preposition but a small clause, which is why it stacks naturally into としては and としても, and why 〜をXとする ("regard X as") is the same construction with the pieces spread out.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" that quotes, and "},{"t":"する"},{"t":" doing what it does in "},{"t":"ことにする"},{"t":": making it so. "},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ"},{"t":"として"},{"t":"来","r":"らい"},{"t":"た"},{"t":" is coming, taken as a doctor.\n\nSo it is not a preposition but a small clause, which is why it stacks naturally into "},{"t":"としては"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"としても"},{"t":", and why 〜"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"X"},{"t":"とする"},{"t":" (\"regard X as\") is the same construction with the pieces spread out."}]'::jsonb),
  ('69059ceeacc5ff5f98d6bf0a8c416de874b0eb8a3097b9d1fea903b8b15e2c7e', 'とって is the te-form of 取る, "to take", taking something as your position.', '[{"t":"とって"},{"t":" is the te-form of "},{"t":"取","r":"と"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", \"to take\", taking something as your position."}]'::jsonb),
  ('02fbe622aee2c631440cbf8e2b95fd2be178f5d355ac2f7e394fd55965457b54', '私にとって is standing where I stand and looking from there. The verb is the everyday 取る, doing the work English does with "from the standpoint of".

That is why にとって wants something whose interests or standpoint can be at stake (a person, a group, a country, even a body, since 日本にとって and 体にとって are perfectly ordinary), and why it pairs with judgements like 大切, 難しい and 必要 rather than with plain descriptions of fact.', '[{"t":"私","r":"わちき"},{"t":"にとって"},{"t":" is standing where I stand and looking from there. The verb is the everyday "},{"t":"取","r":"と"},{"t":"る"},{"t":", doing the work English does with \"from the standpoint of\".\n\nThat is why "},{"t":"にとって"},{"t":" wants something whose interests or standpoint can be at stake (a person, a group, a country, even a body, since "},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん"},{"t":"にとって"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"体","r":"たい"},{"t":"にとって"},{"t":" are perfectly ordinary), and why it pairs with judgements like "},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"難","r":"むずか"},{"t":"しい"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう"},{"t":" rather than with plain descriptions of fact."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4c673eda1406158bb2856a30e873f108160cd1ff6937cd0d0ea018c69a81c688', 'The same construction as といっても with the verb of saying left out entirely. What remains concedes a point and then declines to be moved by it.

子供とて許されない: even granting it is a child. とても is the same とて with も: 「とてもかくても」, "whichever way", which is why it began life carrying negatives. The kinship is real, but the modern meaning has drifted so far that it is invisible.', '[{"t":"The same construction as "},{"t":"といっても"},{"t":" with the verb of saying left out entirely. What remains concedes a point and then declines to be moved by it.\n\n"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども"},{"t":"とて"},{"t":"許","r":"ゆる"},{"t":"されない"},{"t":": even granting it is a child. "},{"t":"とても"},{"t":" is the same "},{"t":"とて"},{"t":" with "},{"t":"も"},{"t":": 「"},{"t":"とてもかくても"},{"t":"」, \"whichever way\", which is why it began life carrying negatives. The kinship is real, but the modern meaning has drifted so far that it is invisible."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d3c9b4a2eabaaa6aa4613adc09f7bb49368c714b9f68024af00de4dc2553b3e8', 'ともに is 共に, "together with", the 共 of 共通 and 共に働く.', '[{"t":"ともに"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"共","r":"とも"},{"t":"に"},{"t":", \"together with\", the "},{"t":"共","r":"とも"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"共通","r":"きょうつう"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"共","r":"とも"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"働","r":"はたら"},{"t":"く"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('61186668f46af67ce41326ce7d269769152fc17290fcd6c5adb531dce4134cb0', 'A plain adverb built on a noun of sharing. とともに names two things as keeping company.

That covers both textbook senses without splitting them: accompanying (家族とともに) and moving in step (年をとるとともに). Company in space and company in change are the same idea, and Japanese did not separate them.', '[{"t":"A plain adverb built on a noun of sharing. "},{"t":"とともに"},{"t":" names two things as keeping company.\n\nThat covers both textbook senses without splitting them: accompanying ("},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく"},{"t":"とともに"},{"t":") and moving in step ("},{"t":"年","r":"とせ"},{"t":"をとるとともに"},{"t":"). Company in space and company in change are the same idea, and Japanese did not separate them."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4c0a97cea69fe2c3a9ba8d075b6fbf9151932d26b5aba834577728a44bf2cb15', 'It asks by what means something came about, and asking that is asking why. なぜ is the plainer native word with no such construction inside it: 何故 is a kanji spelling, not a Sino-Japanese reading.

The literal reading also survives: どうして生きていくのか can genuinely mean "how", not "why". And it is why どうして carries more feeling than なぜ, which merely asks.', '[{"t":"It asks by what means something came about, and asking that is asking why. "},{"t":"なぜ"},{"t":" is the plainer native word with no such construction inside it: "},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"故","r":"こ"},{"t":" is a kanji spelling, not a Sino-Japanese reading.\n\nThe literal reading also survives: "},{"t":"どうして"},{"t":"生","r":"い"},{"t":"きていくのか"},{"t":" can genuinely mean \"how\", not \"why\". And it is why "},{"t":"どうして"},{"t":" carries more feeling than "},{"t":"なぜ"},{"t":", which merely asks."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3a2acf842207647e002709a1830ac7126cf18e8a28a8d3fa106ec737e8a72b82', 'どう asks in what way; に makes it a means; も sweeps every one of them in. With a negative it takes the whole range of methods and rules them out together.

The ど- series itself is not ancient (Old Japanese asked with いか- and いづ-, giving いかにも), but the sweep is the same one that makes 誰も into "nobody" at N4. Sweeping a question word with も and then negating is one of the language''s standard moves, and どうにも is it applied to method.', '[{"t":"どう"},{"t":" asks in what way; "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" makes it a means; "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" sweeps every one of them in. With a negative it takes the whole range of methods and rules them out together.\n\nThe "},{"t":"ど"},{"t":"- series itself is not ancient (Old Japanese asked with "},{"t":"いか"},{"t":"- and "},{"t":"いづ"},{"t":"-, giving "},{"t":"いかにも"},{"t":"), but the sweep is the same one that makes "},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ"},{"t":"も"},{"t":" into \"nobody\" at N4. Sweeping a question word with "},{"t":"も"},{"t":" and then negating is one of the language''s standard moves, and "},{"t":"どうにも"},{"t":" is it applied to method."}]'::jsonb),
  ('dd80dc99dad0caae044a4b2911624e153cd9f3782f74a2fdc94d08164607eb54', 'どころ is ところ, 所, a place, voiced in second position.', '[{"t":"どころ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"ところ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":", a place, voiced in second position."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b0d6e3e70532fb3a0660eae20133b586f8dbcb008491df36429469678d1c2e81', 'The 所 taught at N4 for a moment in time. どころか says the thing named is not even the place where the matter stands.

上手どころか、下手だ dismisses "skilled" as the wrong location entirely and then supplies the right one. That is why どころか always overturns rather than merely qualifying: it has rejected the ground, not adjusted it.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"所","r":"しょ"},{"t":" taught at N4 for a moment in time. "},{"t":"どころか"},{"t":" says the thing named is not even the place where the matter stands.\n\n"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず"},{"t":"どころか"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"下手","r":"したて"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":" dismisses \"skilled\" as the wrong location entirely and then supplies the right one. That is why "},{"t":"どころか"},{"t":" always overturns rather than merely qualifying: it has rejected the ground, not adjusted it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('87db109284fe3646add52279bf387d0100b9d4fb868218d3ed08ebc6f7cf8864', '分かる shares its root with 分ける, "to divide": understanding is telling things apart.', '[{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"かる"},{"t":" shares its root with "},{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"ける"},{"t":", \"to divide\": understanding is telling things apart."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d58592cefad23f9881c96a3c57f7a2f7c271bc74ba50645e60e2d8d26e2a7018', 'Both are formations on the old root 分く, and 分かる is the intransitive one. To understand something is to have it separated out from what it is not: the thing does the coming-apart, in your mind.

That is why it takes が rather than を. 日本語が分かる is "Japanese comes apart for me", with no object to mark. を分かる survives only in pleas about feelings (気持ちを分かってほしい), where what is wanted is the effort, not the state.', '[{"t":"Both are formations on the old root "},{"t":"分","r":"ふん"},{"t":"く"},{"t":", and "},{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"かる"},{"t":" is the intransitive one. To understand something is to have it separated out from what it is not: the thing does the coming-apart, in your mind.\n\nThat is why it takes "},{"t":"が"},{"t":" rather than "},{"t":"を"},{"t":". "},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"かる"},{"t":" is \"Japanese comes apart for me\", with no object to mark. "},{"t":"を"},{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"かる"},{"t":" survives only in pleas about feelings ("},{"t":"気持","r":"きも"},{"t":"ちを"},{"t":"分","r":"わ"},{"t":"かってほしい"},{"t":"), where what is wanted is the effort, not the state."}]'::jsonb),
  ('0b9af2fe0d543995f7c9baa5463c03170bd0ee3b4184283877e10280a114b8fe', 'どんな is どのような worn down, the 様 of ようだ inside it.', '[{"t":"どんな"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"どのような"},{"t":" worn down, the "},{"t":"様","r":"さま"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"ようだ"},{"t":" inside it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('27ef089b73cef5f71600a98cb883fd86c5e7c6c7be7aa98f5768873ec0f530b9', 'どのような contracted to どんな. So the word is the interrogative root ど with the same 様 that gives ようだ, ように and みたい, asking what manner a thing is of.

何の asks which thing rather than which kind, and that is the whole distinction: 何の本 asks what the book is about, どんな本 what sort it is.', '[{"t":"どのような"},{"t":" contracted to "},{"t":"どんな"},{"t":". So the word is the interrogative root "},{"t":"ど"},{"t":" with the same "},{"t":"様","r":"さま"},{"t":" that gives "},{"t":"ようだ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"ように"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"みたい"},{"t":", asking what manner a thing is of.\n\n"},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"の"},{"t":" asks which thing rather than which kind, and that is the whole distinction: "},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":" asks what the book is about, "},{"t":"どんな"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":" what sort it is."}]'::jsonb),
  ('14a34dd5079ffe6f517780ba7b55b2013248de0e4268bdbf8eabc69b10ffafec', 'ない is an adjective, so its past is formed like 寒い''s: the adverbial なく plus the past of ある, contracted to なかった. 寒くあった gave 寒かった by the same route.

So the plain negative past is not a special ending but two ordinary steps, and it is why the polite ませんでした has to do something quite different: ん is not an adjective and has nothing to contract with.', '[{"t":"ない"},{"t":" is an adjective, so its past is formed like "},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"い"},{"t":"''s: the adverbial "},{"t":"なく"},{"t":" plus the past of "},{"t":"ある"},{"t":", contracted to "},{"t":"なかった"},{"t":". "},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"くあった"},{"t":" gave "},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ"},{"t":"かった"},{"t":" by the same route.\n\nSo the plain negative past is not a special ending but two ordinary steps, and it is why the polite "},{"t":"ませんでした"},{"t":" has to do something quite different: "},{"t":"ん"},{"t":" is not an adjective and has nothing to contract with."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e67d6b96198108bdc12a368d2d847b9df007ca711ef331b920236c033d5a6da9', 'なくて is ない in its te-form, inflecting like the adjective it is. ても adds "even", いい judges it acceptable. Every piece is one already taught.

So permission and exemption are the same construction: 食べてもいい allows the act, 食べなくてもいい allows its absence. The casual なくていい simply drops the も.', '[{"t":"なくて"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"ない"},{"t":" in its te-form, inflecting like the adjective it is. "},{"t":"ても"},{"t":" adds \"even\", "},{"t":"いい"},{"t":" judges it acceptable. Every piece is one already taught.\n\nSo permission and exemption are the same construction: "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べてもいい"},{"t":" allows the act, "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べなくてもいい"},{"t":" allows its absence. The casual "},{"t":"なくていい"},{"t":" simply drops the "},{"t":"も"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('7aeb5998965b52710086ba0006deb200dc9d86d47e214bdb9b9a852e6a1ce53a', 'など is worn-down なにと, "what and…", with 等 fitted to it afterwards as a spelling.', '[{"t":"など"},{"t":" is worn-down "},{"t":"なにと"},{"t":", \"what and…\", with "},{"t":"等","r":"ひと"},{"t":" fitted to it afterwards as a spelling."}]'::jsonb),
  ('8f97d6ae89fb99259ac8fe58b964ab0b8209f3f458363d0e8e6728f636cd01ce', 'The derivation is phonetic: なにと contracted to など. 等 is a kanji assigned to write the word later, not the morpheme it descends from; read as Sino-Japanese, 等 would be トウ.

What the original says is still what the particle does. A list trailing off into "and what else" is a list of samples, which is why など follows や so naturally. Worn down further it becomes なんか at N3, where the vagueness has curdled into dismissal.', '[{"t":"The derivation is phonetic: "},{"t":"なにと"},{"t":" contracted to "},{"t":"など"},{"t":". "},{"t":"等","r":"ひと"},{"t":" is a kanji assigned to write the word later, not the morpheme it descends from; read as Sino-Japanese, "},{"t":"等","r":"ひと"},{"t":" would be "},{"t":"トウ"},{"t":".\n\nWhat the original says is still what the particle does. A list trailing off into \"and what else\" is a list of samples, which is why "},{"t":"など"},{"t":" follows "},{"t":"や"},{"t":" so naturally. Worn down further it becomes "},{"t":"なんか"},{"t":" at N3, where the vagueness has curdled into dismissal."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d6c1b9d68e4ee5c5dbe007c9af53f85a6027dde72734ef8f329373ef5f30ed6d', 'ならで is なり plus the で of negation, an older way of saying without being. Adding は makes it a topic and demands the denial that follows.

日本ならではの味 is a taste that, absent Japan, does not occur. The construction is a double denial that lands as exclusivity, which is why it reads as praise rather than as a limit.', '[{"t":"ならで"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"なり"},{"t":" plus the "},{"t":"で"},{"t":" of negation, an older way of saying without being. Adding "},{"t":"は"},{"t":" makes it a topic and demands the denial that follows.\n\n"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん"},{"t":"ならではの"},{"t":"味","r":"あじ"},{"t":" is a taste that, absent Japan, does not occur. The construction is a double denial that lands as exclusivity, which is why it reads as praise rather than as a limit."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4d8aa0465e3322058c10bfb435114aae7801bb34043e5e5bf83e54557aca9c9d', 'ばかり is 計り, from 計る: "to measure".', '[{"t":"ばかり"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"計","r":"けい"},{"t":"り"},{"t":", from "},{"t":"計","r":"はか"},{"t":"る"},{"t":": \"to measure\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('42aa4293100a80eaad005452fef5f6aaa2d5ed138b506aeccce21c9e22dd4845', 'A measure, an amount. That is the sense still visible in 三日ばかり, "about three days", which textbooks list as a separate meaning and which is really the original one.

Narrow "an amount of" to "that amount and no more" and the other uses appear. 遊んでばかりいる is nothing but playing; 食べたばかり is only just eaten, a measure of time so small there is none to spare. One noun, measured tightly.', '[{"t":"A measure, an amount. That is the sense still visible in "},{"t":"三","r":"さん"},{"t":"日","r":"にち"},{"t":"ばかり"},{"t":", \"about three days\", which textbooks list as a separate meaning and which is really the original one.\n\nNarrow \"an amount of\" to \"that amount and no more\" and the other uses appear. "},{"t":"遊","r":"あそ"},{"t":"んでばかりいる"},{"t":" is nothing but playing; "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べたばかり"},{"t":" is only just eaten, a measure of time so small there is none to spare. One noun, measured tightly."}]'::jsonb),
  ('64c7278571df844674c1441615476154adec7e1504fb2e5d0437877d67ad8a45', 'にかけて is 掛ける again: the span is hung between two points.', '[{"t":"にかけて"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"掛","r":"か"},{"t":"ける"},{"t":" again: the span is hung between two points."}]'::jsonb),
  ('fe24e90e272cce6116966844b32fe7b8217631c1eaaf80df7c2b1788a69a03dd', 'The same verb as in 食べかけ and 言いかける. Here what is suspended is a stretch: 月曜から水曜にかけて hangs the period between the two days named.

Unlike から〜まで, which draws hard edges, にかけて never did; its boundaries are approximate, which is why it suits weather, seasons and regions rather than timetables.', '[{"t":"The same verb as in "},{"t":"食","r":"た"},{"t":"べかけ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"言","r":"い"},{"t":"いかける"},{"t":". Here what is suspended is a stretch: "},{"t":"月","r":"げつ"},{"t":"曜から"},{"t":"水曜","r":"すいよう"},{"t":"にかけて"},{"t":" hangs the period between the two days named.\n\nUnlike "},{"t":"から"},{"t":"〜"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":", which draws hard edges, "},{"t":"にかけて"},{"t":" never did; its boundaries are approximate, which is why it suits weather, seasons and regions rather than timetables."}]'::jsonb),
  ('61a87b92eedfad82f7bd6d218e458b808faaaa2b5cab454ea12abf321c9a36ff', 'On a high number or a long span it means "only at": 六十にして始めた, not until sixty. On a small one it means "even at": 一瞬にして消えた, gone in a single instant.

Both are the same construction naming a point and remarking on it. What decides the reading is whether the point is surprisingly late or surprisingly small, and nothing in the grammar tells you which; the noun does.', '[{"t":"On a high number or a long span it means \"only at\": "},{"t":"六","r":"りゅー"},{"t":"十","r":"しー"},{"t":"にして"},{"t":"始","r":"はじ"},{"t":"めた"},{"t":", not until sixty. On a small one it means \"even at\": "},{"t":"一瞬","r":"いっしゅん"},{"t":"にして"},{"t":"消","r":"き"},{"t":"えた"},{"t":", gone in a single instant.\n\nBoth are the same construction naming a point and remarking on it. What decides the reading is whether the point is surprisingly late or surprisingly small, and nothing in the grammar tells you which; the noun does."}]'::jsonb),
  ('29c04e10f46d19930044ddf09995e39d4be3c573f2a61d7ff9406e627929be5b', 'に限らず is 限る with the classical negative: the boundary is not drawn.', '[{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"らず"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" with the classical negative: the boundary is not drawn."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a6da6a405477b3069149362eb5e2e0279e143c51ccb38b00668ef207745d8f6d', '限る is the verb behind かぎり at N3: to set a limit. Negated, it says the limit does not hold.

若者に限らず says the young are not the edge of it. It is the widening twin of に限り, which draws the boundary rather than denying it, and the two are one verb pointed opposite ways.', '[{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" is the verb behind "},{"t":"かぎり"},{"t":" at N3: to set a limit. Negated, it says the limit does not hold.\n\n"},{"t":"若","r":"わか"},{"t":"者","r":"しゃ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"らず"},{"t":" says the young are not the edge of it. It is the widening twin of "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"限","r":"かぎ"},{"t":"り"},{"t":", which draws the boundary rather than denying it, and the two are one verb pointed opposite ways."}]'::jsonb),
  ('cf4b47f4b8ade45b87199efbed0e91e5a496cd21ecb080084c1ef1ec0941a88c', 'Read literally it points at something as a target: 高いのに買った sets the expensiveness up as the thing the buying was done against.

That is where the grievance lives. けれど merely joins two clauses, but のに names one as the mark the other failed to respect, so it always carries the speaker being put out. It is also why のに cannot be followed by a request or an intention: you cannot aim at an outcome and then propose one.', '[{"t":"Read literally it points at something as a target: "},{"t":"高","r":"たか"},{"t":"いのに"},{"t":"買","r":"か"},{"t":"った"},{"t":" sets the expensiveness up as the thing the buying was done against.\n\nThat is where the grievance lives. "},{"t":"けれど"},{"t":" merely joins two clauses, but "},{"t":"のに"},{"t":" names one as the mark the other failed to respect, so it always carries the speaker being put out. It is also why "},{"t":"のに"},{"t":" cannot be followed by a request or an intention: you cannot aim at an outcome and then propose one."}]'::jsonb),
  ('5ac2599fcaf517257ed5bdc51b394dd1db3aed8cb5fb142c3ca780e8a9cc8b5f', 'はずみ is 弾み, from 弾む: "to bounce".', '[{"t":"はずみ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"弾","r":"だん"},{"t":"み"},{"t":", from "},{"t":"弾","r":"はず"},{"t":"む"},{"t":": \"to bounce\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d43b9fe6f519754440ef798888b3f192f03b4f4b960ba28230c3622c8e02c068', 'ボールが弾む is a ball bouncing; 話が弾む is a conversation that takes off. The noun is momentum.

So 転んだはずみに is on the bounce of falling, the accident carried on into something else by its own momentum. That is why the pattern always describes something unintended: a bounce is not aimed.', '[{"t":"ボールが"},{"t":"弾","r":"はず"},{"t":"む"},{"t":" is a ball bouncing; "},{"t":"話","r":"わ"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"弾","r":"はず"},{"t":"む"},{"t":" is a conversation that takes off. The noun is momentum.\n\nSo "},{"t":"転","r":"ころ"},{"t":"んだはずみに"},{"t":" is on the bounce of falling, the accident carried on into something else by its own momentum. That is why the pattern always describes something unintended: a bounce is not aimed."}]'::jsonb),
  ('abb11b6698ecfa990526528ee79f0e9a35fecdd9741b43bea67a4a926284a167', 'べく plus あら plus ず, contracted. It is the negative branch of the same auxiliary that gives べき and べく.

