  ('12177c7c5e60bb24c7edcfac2422cbec0cd981f9b16b51e0db245743b5f78368', 'A whole hedged question compressed into two syllables: に + や + あらむ became やらむ, やらう, やら. The ら is what is left of あらむ, not the ら of 彼ら.

That buried question is why やら suits complaint and fluster. 雨やら風やら names two things out of a mess nobody is trying to count, each offered as a "might it be", and 何やら is the same hedge with nothing listed at all.', '[{"t":"A whole hedged question compressed into two syllables: "},{"t":"に"},{"t":" + "},{"t":"や"},{"t":" + "},{"t":"あらむ"},{"t":" became "},{"t":"やらむ"},{"t":", "},{"t":"やらう"},{"t":", "},{"t":"やら"},{"t":". The "},{"t":"ら"},{"t":" is what is left of "},{"t":"あらむ"},{"t":", not the "},{"t":"ら"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ"},{"t":"ら"},{"t":".\n\nThat buried question is why "},{"t":"やら"},{"t":" suits complaint and fluster. "},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ"},{"t":"やら"},{"t":"風","r":"かぜ"},{"t":"やら"},{"t":" names two things out of a mess nobody is trying to count, each offered as a \"might it be\", and "},{"t":"何","r":"なに"},{"t":"やら"},{"t":" is the same hedge with nothing listed at all."}]'::jsonb),
  ('addacbed655d0f040495b32c2a61f426098aab00d61a82271acf1835aba648d3', 'The と is the quotative one: it takes 行こう, the intention, and hands it to する. So the phrase is literally "do the intending".

That explains both readings without splitting them. An intention acted on is an attempt (開けようとしたが開かなかった); an intention about to be acted on is being on the point of it (出かけようとしたとき). What differs is only how far the doing got.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"と"},{"t":" is the quotative one: it takes "},{"t":"行","r":"い"},{"t":"こう"},{"t":", the intention, and hands it to "},{"t":"する"},{"t":". So the phrase is literally \"do the intending\".\n\nThat explains both readings without splitting them. An intention acted on is an attempt ("},{"t":"開","r":"あ"},{"t":"けようとしたが"},{"t":"開","r":"あ"},{"t":"かなかった"},{"t":"); an intention about to be acted on is being on the point of it ("},{"t":"出","r":"で"},{"t":"かけようとしたとき"},{"t":"). What differs is only how far the doing got."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4b94ef853bf9d6e8cd3c9084838723cbe6ad7dab6d9092768741b2b25897a7e3', 'ように is the noun 様 with に: "in such a way that it comes about".', '[{"t":"ように"},{"t":" is the noun "},{"t":"様","r":"さま"},{"t":" with "},{"t":"に"},{"t":": \"in such a way that it comes about\"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('ccb9685dc7e612e80fc72a2837f75b3871a443893f19a32a704ddd72aec403d3', 'ために names a purpose you intend. ように names a manner you hope things fall into, and the difference is doing real work in the grammar.

It is why ように takes the verbs you cannot simply decide (potentials, negatives, things happening to other people), while ために takes the ones you can. 聞こえるように大きな声で話す, because being audible is not something you do; 勉強するために, because studying is. Same speaker, same intention, two constructions, and the verb decides which.', '[{"t":"ために"},{"t":" names a purpose you intend. "},{"t":"ように"},{"t":" names a manner you hope things fall into, and the difference is doing real work in the grammar.\n\nIt is why "},{"t":"ように"},{"t":" takes the verbs you cannot simply decide (potentials, negatives, things happening to other people), while "},{"t":"ために"},{"t":" takes the ones you can. "},{"t":"聞","r":"き"},{"t":"こえるように"},{"t":"大","r":"おお"},{"t":"きな"},{"t":"声","r":"しょう"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"話","r":"はな"},{"t":"す"},{"t":", because being audible is not something you do; "},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう"},{"t":"するために"},{"t":", because studying is. Same speaker, same intention, two constructions, and the verb decides which."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d79240e258143afa3f702bb2f97eb9f8eee2a85fffceb2fb98e6805ced608c30', 'The volitional is intention. にも adds "even". So 出かけようにも is "even meaning to go out", and the potential negative that must follow supplies what stops it.

