  ('5ffc3bbb30d6a654f044cccb6f972aba5c81d5879dd077a49b6558390a1b287c', '始末 is 始 plus 末: the beginning and the end, the whole course of a thing.', '[{"t":"始末","r":"しまつ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"始","r":"はじ"},{"t":" plus "},{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":": the beginning and the end, the whole course of a thing."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d3e3d9430eb669c0de1455e958cd9641ee5eef2628090d2a50dedc09bd1ba2a2', 'The noun means how something turned out, and it survives in 始末書, the written account of an incident you are answerable for.

Because it names the entire course, 始末だ always follows a stated sequence of events and delivers the verdict on it. And because you only write a 始末書 when things went wrong, the verdict is always bad.', '[{"t":"The noun means how something turned out, and it survives in "},{"t":"始末","r":"しまつ"},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":", the written account of an incident you are answerable for.\n\nBecause it names the entire course, "},{"t":"始末","r":"しまつ"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":" always follows a stated sequence of events and delivers the verdict on it. And because you only write a "},{"t":"始末","r":"しまつ"},{"t":"書","r":"か"},{"t":" when things went wrong, the verdict is always bad."}]'::jsonb),
  ('14756b5f228ed1a113e11db88cf04576b388def98df3ab758ffafbb364e1e40e', '当たる is "to strike, to hit", the moment something lands.', '[{"t":"当","r":"あ"},{"t":"たる"},{"t":" is \"to strike, to hit\", the moment something lands."}]'::jsonb),
  ('35149aa3af23cfcdc2aa3e006e728daa7c541e948a54a74b880959dfd8f332c2', 'The verb in 当たり前 and くじに当たる. に当たって marks the point of impact between a person and an occasion.

That is why it takes significant beginnings (開会に当たって, 就職に当たって) and not routine ones. Something has to be substantial enough to strike against.', '[{"t":"The verb in "},{"t":"当","r":"あ"},{"t":"たり"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"くじに"},{"t":"当","r":"あ"},{"t":"たる"},{"t":". "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"当","r":"あ"},{"t":"たって"},{"t":" marks the point of impact between a person and an occasion.\n\nThat is why it takes significant beginnings ("},{"t":"開会","r":"かいかい"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"当","r":"あ"},{"t":"たって"},{"t":", "},{"t":"就職","r":"しゅうしょく"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"当","r":"あ"},{"t":"たって"},{"t":") and not routine ones. Something has to be substantial enough to strike against."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d65fc3b7b0c476bb2c18fbedc6e45848607002477bd95b97ad59398b2129fdaf', '応じる is "to respond, to answer", the 応 of 応答 and 反応.', '[{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":"じる"},{"t":" is \"to respond, to answer\", the "},{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":"答","r":"こた"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"反応","r":"はんのう"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('b9f8e53a20fc115b0fb1fbd5cf371784f03162c95df43dded67d8ecc1e0ec23d', '呼びかけに応じる is answering a call. に応じて makes one thing the answer to another.

Both textbook senses are that one act. Responding to a request is 要望に応じて; varying with a condition is 収入に応じて, where the amount answers the income. Something adjusts itself to fit, which is exactly what responding means.', '[{"t":"呼","r":"よ"},{"t":"びかけに"},{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":"じる"},{"t":" is answering a call. "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":"じて"},{"t":" makes one thing the answer to another.\n\nBoth textbook senses are that one act. Responding to a request is "},{"t":"要望","r":"ようぼう"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":"じて"},{"t":"; varying with a condition is "},{"t":"収入","r":"しゅうにゅう"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"応","r":"おう"},{"t":"じて"},{"t":", where the amount answers the income. Something adjusts itself to fit, which is exactly what responding means."}]'::jsonb),
  ('608de515c9f4eb0704dfb8f133847c3ed54728c6db357b0aa16102df21f27dd2', '抜き is 抜く, "to pull out": the thing is extracted, not merely absent.', '[{"t":"抜","r":"ぬ"},{"t":"き"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"抜","r":"ぬ"},{"t":"く"},{"t":", \"to pull out\": the thing is extracted, not merely absent."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3cce92415c948f9aa18a9b116d764f4ad639e11b7b2dbff40fd54926dc8ef6aa', '歯を抜く is pulling a tooth; ワサビ抜き is sushi with the wasabi taken out. Something that was expected has been removed.

That is the difference from なしに, which merely says a thing was not there. 冗談抜きで works because a joke was in play and has been pulled; it would make no sense about something never present.', '[{"t":"歯","r":"し"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"抜","r":"ぬ"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is pulling a tooth; "},{"t":"ワサビ"},{"t":"抜","r":"ぬ"},{"t":"き"},{"t":" is sushi with the wasabi taken out. Something that was expected has been removed.\n\nThat is the difference from "},{"t":"なしに"},{"t":", which merely says a thing was not there. "},{"t":"冗談","r":"じょうだん"},{"t":"抜","r":"ぬ"},{"t":"きで"},{"t":" works because a joke was in play and has been pulled; it would make no sense about something never present."}]'::jsonb),
  ('be56032812bf1b25bd7f15749d5471c3788bd6d63f18b73e317491b1698644cd', '末 is the tip or far end of something, the thin end of a branch.', '[{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":" is the tip or far end of something, the thin end of a branch."}]'::jsonb),
  ('881c15cce2a18525ea25201b9edc2abcdcaaf23ea899bb3b692e0a742dfe767c', 'The noun in 末っ子 and 週末. 末に puts the outcome at the far end of a long stretch.

So it needs the stretch. 悩んだ末に決めた works because the worrying went on; a quick decision has no 末 to arrive at. Unlike あげく, 末 passes no judgement on how it turned out.', '[{"t":"The noun in "},{"t":"末","r":"すえ"},{"t":"っ"},{"t":"子","r":"こ"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"週","r":"しゅう"},{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":". "},{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":" puts the outcome at the far end of a long stretch.\n\nSo it needs the stretch. "},{"t":"悩","r":"なや"},{"t":"んだ"},{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"決","r":"き"},{"t":"めた"},{"t":" works because the worrying went on; a quick decision has no "},{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":" to arrive at. Unlike "},{"t":"あげく"},{"t":", "},{"t":"末","r":"うれ"},{"t":" passes no judgement on how it turned out."}]'::jsonb),
  ('13f27cfc3c21df027dd938b6a7f5122654d141052d94b04290ad70dfdf1e042e', '本 means "origin, base", and long thin things are counted by it because a tree trunk is one.', '[{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":" means \"origin, base\", and long thin things are counted by it because a tree trunk is one."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f8d1b8caf637fd4dff013b89f37dbac84007ec03a5ade41d2bac787deda953f9', 'The 本 of 日本 and 本当 is a root or a foundation, not a book. A trunk is the base a tree grows from, and the counter spread from trunks to everything long and slender.

Which is why it now counts pens, bottles, umbrellas and roads, and then stretches to telephone calls, films and home runs, things with length in time rather than space. The sound changes are worth learning as a set: いっぽん, さんぼん, ろっぽん.', '[{"t":"The "},{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"本当","r":"ほんとう"},{"t":" is a root or a foundation, not a book. A trunk is the base a tree grows from, and the counter spread from trunks to everything long and slender.\n\nWhich is why it now counts pens, bottles, umbrellas and roads, and then stretches to telephone calls, films and home runs, things with length in time rather than space. The sound changes are worth learning as a set: "},{"t":"いっぽん"},{"t":", "},{"t":"さんぼん"},{"t":", "},{"t":"ろっぽん"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3cf0665026ccb93a698529ec218d13f0c6f1fef83220a41e080754baaa339681', '枚 named a small piece of wood in Chinese, and in Japan was matched to the native ひら, a flat thin thing.', '[{"t":"枚"},{"t":" named a small piece of wood in Chinese, and in Japan was matched to the native "},{"t":"ひら"},{"t":", a flat thin thing."}]'::jsonb),
  ('4e11524df4bf53009d3ef4d552fda0c76bb0531f654c1137074f48bca6c4d447', 'Chinese 枚 was a near-general classifier, close in scope to 个. Japanese narrowed it to thin flat things, and the narrowing shows early: 上代 texts already read the character as ひら, the ひら of 花びら, and medieval documents use it mostly for boards.

The story that reads the flat sense off the 木 radical is folk etymology, and the specialisation is a Japanese development rather than an inherited one. What makes it worth learning first is the sound: unlike 本 and 匹 it takes no changes at all (いちまい through じゅうまい, every one regular).', '[{"t":"Chinese "},{"t":"枚"},{"t":" was a near-general classifier, close in scope to "},{"t":"个"},{"t":". Japanese narrowed it to thin flat things, and the narrowing shows early: "},{"t":"上","r":"うえ"},{"t":"代","r":"か"},{"t":" texts already read the character as "},{"t":"ひら"},{"t":", the "},{"t":"ひら"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"花","r":"はな"},{"t":"びら"},{"t":", and medieval documents use it mostly for boards.\n\nThe story that reads the flat sense off the "},{"t":"木","r":"き"},{"t":" radical is folk etymology, and the specialisation is a Japanese development rather than an inherited one. What makes it worth learning first is the sound: unlike "},{"t":"本","r":"ほん"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"匹"},{"t":" it takes no changes at all ("},{"t":"いちまい"},{"t":" through "},{"t":"じゅうまい"},{"t":", every one regular)."}]'::jsonb),
  ('769d7e2034acc9748ea6dc5027705d2e8ce256de5ca9f47af58caf0aa72e6824', '極まりない is the noun 極まり (a stopping place) plus 無い.', '[{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まりない"},{"t":" is the noun "},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まり"},{"t":" (a stopping place) plus "},{"t":"無","r":"ぶ"},{"t":"い"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('5a6bd77f540cfb24edc7f49534aa2b41ab4e147bd8dddbc8c46bf554c71e8c83', '極まる is "to reach the furthest point", the 極 of 北極 and 極端, and 極まり is the point reached. 失礼極まりない says the rudeness has no such point, so it goes on without bound.

Read that way the puzzle disappears: the ない is doing ordinary negative work on a noun, not bleached negation on a verb. It is also why 失礼極まる means much the same thing rather than the opposite; one says the extreme is reached, the other that no bound exists.', '[{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まる"},{"t":" is \"to reach the furthest point\", the "},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"北極","r":"ほっきょく"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"極端","r":"きょくたん"},{"t":", and "},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まり"},{"t":" is the point reached. "},{"t":"失礼","r":"しつれい"},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まりない"},{"t":" says the rudeness has no such point, so it goes on without bound.\n\nRead that way the puzzle disappears: the "},{"t":"ない"},{"t":" is doing ordinary negative work on a noun, not bleached negation on a verb. It is also why "},{"t":"失礼","r":"しつれい"},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まる"},{"t":" means much the same thing rather than the opposite; one says the extreme is reached, the other that no bound exists."}]'::jsonb),
  ('22c9f983d2b2f04444a04c3511f3c7f5adb05e20a555ac162492716d053299bb', '極み is the noun from the 極まる of 極まりない, the summit itself.', '[{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"み"},{"t":" is the noun from the "},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まる"},{"t":" of "},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まりない"},{"t":", the summit itself."}]'::jsonb),
  ('09f0908730b4b0d538602f0b0bf3cd434d248c875cce72b2ef02a878d6ce2cca', 'Where 極まりない denies that a peak exists, の極み names it and puts the thing on top of it. 贅沢の極み is the very peak of luxury.

One kanji, two patterns, opposite constructions: the negated verb says there is no limit, the plain noun says here is the limit and this is it.', '[{"t":"Where "},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"まりない"},{"t":" denies that a peak exists, "},{"t":"の"},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"み"},{"t":" names it and puts the thing on top of it. "},{"t":"贅沢","r":"ぜいたく"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"極","r":"ごく"},{"t":"み"},{"t":" is the very peak of luxury.\n\nOne kanji, two patterns, opposite constructions: the negated verb says there is no limit, the plain noun says here is the limit and this is it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('68731eeb4c8d6353ef5512619482bcc24c5e8a2e0a8768c0d9d5b04f791eaec3', '次第 is 次 plus 第: order, sequence, the way things fall out.', '[{"t":"次第","r":"しだい"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"次","r":"じ"},{"t":" plus "},{"t":"第"},{"t":": order, sequence, the way things fall out."}]'::jsonb),
  ('f79c4e2bdbb1665579eaa0587fc599afc902928447d611d4155d626b1dcc7840', 'Both kanji mean sequence. The noun names how matters are arranged, and every use follows from that.

着き次第 is "in the order following arrival", hence as soon as. 天気次第 is according to how things fall. And ご説明した次第です, the one that baffles people, is simply "such is the sequence of it", a formal way of closing an account.', '[{"t":"Both kanji mean sequence. The noun names how matters are arranged, and every use follows from that.\n\n"},{"t":"着","r":"き"},{"t":"き"},{"t":"次第","r":"しだい"},{"t":" is \"in the order following arrival\", hence as soon as. "},{"t":"天気","r":"てんき"},{"t":"次第","r":"しだい"},{"t":" is according to how things fall. And "},{"t":"ご"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい"},{"t":"した"},{"t":"次第","r":"しだい"},{"t":"です"},{"t":", the one that baffles people, is simply \"such is the sequence of it\", a formal way of closing an account."}]'::jsonb),
  ('75dc2232af72fa12887776403c29874e57555c7591d56f18b44829d570cfec53', '気味 is 気 plus 味: the feel and the flavour of a thing.', '[{"t":"気味","r":"ぎみ"},{"t":" is "},{"t":"気","r":"げ"},{"t":" plus "},{"t":"味","r":"あじ"},{"t":": the feel and the flavour of a thing."}]'::jsonb),
  ('675bc923ede24f58e1a760873c1330b3443f055cc4552a1518a5470941cee9ae', '気 is the same 気 as in 元気 and 天気; 味 is taste. Together they name a slight quality detectable in something.

Which is why 気味 is always a small amount and usually an unwelcome one: 風邪気味 is a touch of a cold, 遅れ気味 is running a little late. A trace is what you notice when something is going slightly wrong.', '[{"t":"気","r":"げ"},{"t":" is the same "},{"t":"気","r":"げ"},{"t":" as in "},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき"},{"t":" and "},{"t":"天気","r":"てんき"},{"t":"; "},{"t":"味","r":"あじ"},{"t":" is taste. Together they name a slight quality detectable in something.\n\nWhich is why "},{"t":"気味","r":"ぎみ"},{"t":" is always a small amount and usually an unwelcome one: "},{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ"},{"t":"気味","r":"ぎみ"},{"t":" is a touch of a cold, "},{"t":"遅","r":"おく"},{"t":"れ"},{"t":"気味","r":"ぎみ"},{"t":" is running a little late. A trace is what you notice when something is going slightly wrong."}]'::jsonb),
  ('8bd9485ca8b8a4971f43ac92c853b02a3982c1962e169af4f47aa36d53b1ccfb', '沿う is "to run alongside": a road following a river.', '[{"t":"沿","r":"そ"},{"t":"う"},{"t":" is \"to run alongside\": a road following a river."}]'::jsonb),
  ('37500dbb54a306ee44c04e06ae09c02018af46c36c966040976615236f78940b', '川に沿って歩く is the literal use, and it is still the commonest one. The pattern keeps the picture of two things lying parallel.

Which is why に沿って covers both a physical line (道に沿って) and an abstract one (方針に沿って): a policy is something you keep beside rather than something you are fastened to. That is the difference from に基づいて.', '[{"t":"川","r":"かわ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"沿","r":"そ"},{"t":"って"},{"t":"歩","r":"ある"},{"t":"く"},{"t":" is the literal use, and it is still the commonest one. The pattern keeps the picture of two things lying parallel.\n\nWhich is why "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"沿","r":"そ"},{"t":"って"},{"t":" covers both a physical line ("},{"t":"道","r":"たお"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"沿","r":"そ"},{"t":"って"},{"t":") and an abstract one ("},{"t":"方針","r":"ほうしん"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"沿","r":"そ"},{"t":"って"},{"t":"): a policy is something you keep beside rather than something you are fastened to. That is the difference from "},{"t":"に"},{"t":"基","r":"もと"},{"t":"づいて"},{"t":"."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e03b2dddefb62dddaa79162624b6123f22de0de184bc42008bdf0b6b13e4305a', '渡る is "to cross over", the verb for getting from one side to the other.', '[{"t":"渡","r":"わた"},{"t":"る"},{"t":" is \"to cross over\", the verb for getting from one side to the other."}]'::jsonb),
  ('9ac580a5a4d8adf453ffcca1984f6e91f6f28b5a5a93635d1290da578a4eec7b', '橋を渡る, to cross a bridge. にわたって stretches the sentence across a span the way a crossing stretches over water.

